7592186415104 :step/next "step-d88e62ca-aba2-5795-a208-8e8ba9cb91b6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d88e62ca-aba2-5795-a208-8e8ba9cb91b6" :step/id #uuid "d88e62ca-aba2-5795-a208-8e8ba9cb91b6" :step/content 17592186415107 :step/next "step-1911f355-c319-5a82-939d-0d7b2b9be6b0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1911f355-c319-5a82-939d-0d7b2b9be6b0" :step/id #uuid "1911f355-c319-5a82-939d-0d7b2b9be6b0" :step/content 17592186259586 :step/next "step-ce79cbc6-e7a2-5282-9005-4ff166707378" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ce79cbc6-e7a2-5282-9005-4ff166707378" :step/id #uuid "ce79cbc6-e7a2-5282-9005-4ff166707378" :step/content 17592186259588 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5f4136a2-4487-503b-8a03-26f4549cc680"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082904 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\n \n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(4)) & ={f}^{-1}(-1) \\\\ 4 & ={f}^{-1}(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-606d0246-f6d2-5866-ae11-21bc32e2c786" "question-8bc675ed-3657-5d25-80e1-2c0715c7f4ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082905 :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(-4)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ -4 & ={f}^{-1}(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(3) = -4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2ed610a-45fe-59d0-b6a4-e6d3deb1450a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082906 :question/problem "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-3) = 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fe2ad98f-6565-54a7-a35e-058849653046" "question-b9b48ed6-0697-5f61-bbb9-09dbd79829b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082907 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x.\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =4x-2k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =4f(x)-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${\\color{blue}f(x)} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{x}{4}-2}$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =4f(x)-2k \\\\  & =4(\\frac{x}{4}-2)-2k \\\\  & =x-8-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ we also know that $g(f(x)) = x.$ Equating the above expression with $x$ and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-8-2k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-8-2k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -8-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083878 :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c884e34e0fd8b310984c2197ff0ea606033d00d63a0c6e0e04f0c993f519755.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is ${f}^{-1}(4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function ${f}^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/a0/a0194a90466532c723c904d5ef9fea394e976b8b1f5287f066d5843f575e762e.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that ${f}^{-1}(4)=5.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have:\n\n$$\nf(5)=4\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(5)) & ={f}^{-1}(4) \\\\ 5 & ={f}^{-1}(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(4)=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082199 :answer-field/correct 17592186082202 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-5f4136a2-4487-503b-8a03-26f4549cc680" :question/id #uuid "5f4136a2-4487-503b-8a03-26f4549cc680" :question/math-academy-id "q-66451" :question/problem "![](images/72/724d629662e879a2d57f920eb4fe8c3cfc44f9f755d2ae5dca10d5605bab0f9d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is ${f}^{-1}(1)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function ${f}^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/17/17b769a94abc2035d5d24e82c65d9c8fd6252152482826c3a34e8708d246c213.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that ${f}^{-1}(1)=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(-2)) & ={f}^{-1}(1) \\\\ -2 & ={f}^{-1}(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8ccd2018-b672-5b43-8607-779f09001359"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8ccd2018-b672-5b43-8607-779f09001359" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ccd2018-b672-5b43-8607-779f09001359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18677a6e-733f-57df-ad8b-94b3ad04d939" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18677a6e-733f-57df-ad8b-94b3ad04d939" "answer-c86086d0-56f2-5d34-8aaf-874cf930f4f0" "answer-c1a5d777-a1f7-5ae6-bf06-546e5547251f" "answer-4241441a-f5ab-5186-a481-f71e7d7cb1c5" "answer-a9b8ba7f-e500-5ec5-ac08-8abb17b7a985"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18677a6e-733f-57df-ad8b-94b3ad04d939" :answer/id #uuid "18677a6e-733f-57df-ad8b-94b3ad04d939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c86086d0-56f2-5d34-8aaf-874cf930f4f0" :answer/id #uuid "c86086d0-56f2-5d34-8aaf-874cf930f4f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1a5d777-a1f7-5ae6-bf06-546e5547251f" :answer/id #uuid "c1a5d777-a1f7-5ae6-bf06-546e5547251f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4241441a-f5ab-5186-a481-f71e7d7cb1c5" :answer/id #uuid "4241441a-f5ab-5186-a481-f71e7d7cb1c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9b8ba7f-e500-5ec5-ac08-8abb17b7a985" :answer/id #uuid "a9b8ba7f-e500-5ec5-ac08-8abb17b7a985" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-606d0246-f6d2-5866-ae11-21bc32e2c786" :question/id #uuid "606d0246-f6d2-5866-ae11-21bc32e2c786" :question/math-academy-id "q-5735" :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-10$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nGiven the function table above, what does ${g}^{-1}(-3)$ equal?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we have:\n\n$$\ng(-1)=-3\n$$\n\nApplying ${g}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${g}^{-1}(g(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{-1}(g(-1)) & ={g}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ -1 & ={g}^{-1}(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${g}^{-1}(-3)=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40720521-50da-5e32-aa83-58bfa144974e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40720521-50da-5e32-aa83-58bfa144974e" :answer-field/id #uuid "40720521-50da-5e32-aa83-58bfa144974e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-394bab23-962a-51c8-833e-80f9b7eb8de0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-394bab23-962a-51c8-833e-80f9b7eb8de0" "answer-ec45a8c3-09c8-5768-a5f9-465c7c9a0e97" "answer-369626a2-75ff-5dc1-bd40-9972ef31265b" "answer-27551c53-6d8d-5114-b9f2-212782d87f9a" "answer-7bb50109-fcce-5941-824a-7239d998b5a8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-394bab23-962a-51c8-833e-80f9b7eb8de0" :answer/id #uuid "394bab23-962a-51c8-833e-80f9b7eb8de0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec45a8c3-09c8-5768-a5f9-465c7c9a0e97" :answer/id #uuid "ec45a8c3-09c8-5768-a5f9-465c7c9a0e97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-369626a2-75ff-5dc1-bd40-9972ef31265b" :answer/id #uuid "369626a2-75ff-5dc1-bd40-9972ef31265b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27551c53-6d8d-5114-b9f2-212782d87f9a" :answer/id #uuid "27551c53-6d8d-5114-b9f2-212782d87f9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bb50109-fcce-5941-824a-7239d998b5a8" :answer/id #uuid "7bb50109-fcce-5941-824a-7239d998b5a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8bc675ed-3657-5d25-80e1-2c0715c7f4ff" :question/id #uuid "8bc675ed-3657-5d25-80e1-2c0715c7f4ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-78657" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\ng(-\\sqrt{2})=4,\\,g(\\sqrt{3})=-7,\\,g(4)=-6,\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${g}^{-1}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have:\n\n$$\ng(-\\sqrt{2})=4\n$$\n\nApplying ${g}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${g}^{-1}(g(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{-1}(g(-\\sqrt{2})) & ={g}^{-1}(4) \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & ={g}^{-1}(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${g}^{-1}(4)=-\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8819bc91-0a41-5027-b286-62afcab345f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8819bc91-0a41-5027-b286-62afcab345f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "8819bc91-0a41-5027-b286-62afcab345f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b24f4550-672c-53ac-834f-c8084891337f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae98661d-734e-5061-bb02-eaa3e66cc6dd" "answer-03abb4dd-f562-58eb-a686-a3b8d32c16e5" "answer-b24f4550-672c-53ac-834f-c8084891337f" "answer-b2c69d7f-b592-5e39-877d-8426f6aa9efe" "answer-36c5ef30-80ac-584c-9b22-c1975a553bb8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae98661d-734e-5061-bb02-eaa3e66cc6dd" :answer/id #uuid "ae98661d-734e-5061-bb02-eaa3e66cc6dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03abb4dd-f562-58eb-a686-a3b8d32c16e5" :answer/id #uuid "03abb4dd-f562-58eb-a686-a3b8d32c16e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b24f4550-672c-53ac-834f-c8084891337f" :answer/id #uuid "b24f4550-672c-53ac-834f-c8084891337f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2c69d7f-b592-5e39-877d-8426f6aa9efe" :answer/id #uuid "b2c69d7f-b592-5e39-877d-8426f6aa9efe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36c5ef30-80ac-584c-9b22-c1975a553bb8" :answer/id #uuid "36c5ef30-80ac-584c-9b22-c1975a553bb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083663 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9360c7c07ecc0d78bc57fbcb56cc5ca3b799109ee48747bd4f651ad1209cd63.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of ${f}^{-1}(1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $C$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,1).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=1\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(1) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080909 :answer-field/correct 17592186080912 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2ed610a-45fe-59d0-b6a4-e6d3deb1450a" :question/id #uuid "f2ed610a-45fe-59d0-b6a4-e6d3deb1450a" :question/math-academy-id "q-78645" :question/problem "![](images/18/1822626e3be735821c87aedbebfe29c2ede05a38bc71f9ea75e77f643a885c15.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,D,$ and $E$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of ${f}^{-1}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,3).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5992071f-e276-50f8-9e37-6cca67995420"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5992071f-e276-50f8-9e37-6cca67995420" :answer-field/id #uuid "5992071f-e276-50f8-9e37-6cca67995420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f7c8c46-db0a-53b7-87f1-adc3c5a25449" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fd4dc4b2-b60c-5f66-b29d-4cfdfc454806" "answer-99bb021f-fbc0-5b2c-8519-b1966d7eafcf" "answer-3e16ecb3-38e9-5381-b8b0-f1b6d0bd08fb" "answer-5f7c8c46-db0a-53b7-87f1-adc3c5a25449" "answer-110c3b30-4ace-5802-aa7a-d2b381f8d9ae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd4dc4b2-b60c-5f66-b29d-4cfdfc454806" :answer/id #uuid "fd4dc4b2-b60c-5f66-b29d-4cfdfc454806" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99bb021f-fbc0-5b2c-8519-b1966d7eafcf" :answer/id #uuid "99bb021f-fbc0-5b2c-8519-b1966d7eafcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e16ecb3-38e9-5381-b8b0-f1b6d0bd08fb" :answer/id #uuid "3e16ecb3-38e9-5381-b8b0-f1b6d0bd08fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f7c8c46-db0a-53b7-87f1-adc3c5a25449" :answer/id #uuid "5f7c8c46-db0a-53b7-87f1-adc3c5a25449" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-110c3b30-4ace-5802-aa7a-d2b381f8d9ae" :answer/id #uuid "110c3b30-4ace-5802-aa7a-d2b381f8d9ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fe2ad98f-6565-54a7-a35e-058849653046" :question/id #uuid "fe2ad98f-6565-54a7-a35e-058849653046" :question/math-academy-id "q-158082" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=x-10$ and $g(x)=x+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=x-10$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =f(x)+k \\\\  & =x-10+k \\\\  & =x-10+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-10+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-10+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -10+k & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75360117-0eb5-556b-ac7b-98f14e9e1ad8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75360117-0eb5-556b-ac7b-98f14e9e1ad8" :answer-field/id #uuid "75360117-0eb5-556b-ac7b-98f14e9e1ad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d4cca13-c815-58ba-9fcd-6e34d17587cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1aed1dff-7ca9-52e0-8b89-7b430c555090" "answer-f785a993-a2b9-5076-9a30-a909aadb76e0" "answer-46b5fbdf-7881-58bb-8ed4-5e95b123f475" "answer-7d4cca13-c815-58ba-9fcd-6e34d17587cd" "answer-a8ed0166-dc33-50e1-bf91-10ba85cd775f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aed1dff-7ca9-52e0-8b89-7b430c555090" :answer/id #uuid "1aed1dff-7ca9-52e0-8b89-7b430c555090" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f785a993-a2b9-5076-9a30-a909aadb76e0" :answer/id #uuid "f785a993-a2b9-5076-9a30-a909aadb76e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46b5fbdf-7881-58bb-8ed4-5e95b123f475" :answer/id #uuid "46b5fbdf-7881-58bb-8ed4-5e95b123f475" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d4cca13-c815-58ba-9fcd-6e34d17587cd" :answer/id #uuid "7d4cca13-c815-58ba-9fcd-6e34d17587cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8ed0166-dc33-50e1-bf91-10ba85cd775f" :answer/id #uuid "a8ed0166-dc33-50e1-bf91-10ba85cd775f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083662 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ and $g(x)=3x+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace everxy occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =3x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k \\\\  & =3(\\frac{x}{3}+7)+k \\\\  & =x+21+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+21+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+21+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 21+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080903 :answer-field/correct 17592186080908 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b9b48ed6-0697-5f61-bbb9-09dbd79829b3" :question/id #uuid "b9b48ed6-0697-5f61-bbb9-09dbd79829b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-158088" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ and $g(x)=kx.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =kx \\\\ g(f(x)) & =kf(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =kf(x) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{x}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{8}x & =x \\\\ \\frac{k}{8}\\cancel{x} & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ \\frac{k}{8} & =1 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-191fb7c8-e3c8-5ad7-adce-9b609ebb5aad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-191fb7c8-e3c8-5ad7-adce-9b609ebb5aad" :answer-field/id #uuid "191fb7c8-e3c8-5ad7-adce-9b609ebb5aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2d3e687-f95d-56c9-991e-e43f37331b40" "answer-df3c390d-6642-54ee-b5ac-caf9d0550e22" "answer-ee98e3cd-e735-5e78-950d-efe59ed9913a" "answer-bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" "answer-12c03d51-e827-5c7e-b631-35a43305197f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2d3e687-f95d-56c9-991e-e43f37331b40" :answer/id #uuid "c2d3e687-f95d-56c9-991e-e43f37331b40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df3c390d-6642-54ee-b5ac-caf9d0550e22" :answer/id #uuid "df3c390d-6642-54ee-b5ac-caf9d0550e22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee98e3cd-e735-5e78-950d-efe59ed9913a" :answer/id #uuid "ee98e3cd-e735-5e78-950d-efe59ed9913a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer/id #uuid "bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12c03d51-e827-5c7e-b631-35a43305197f" :answer/id #uuid "12c03d51-e827-5c7e-b631-35a43305197f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084499 :tutorial/content "Consider the mapping diagram of the function $f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/41/415b9ce71454f2aec9a2fc678c79f1d34fbb16468fbac50fc62b76283b9c209a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's now reverse the arrows, as follows:\n\n![](images/80/801b3eca4820b110c2145bd13fbf1faddbb0db83905869410cad1dc8a32f4e83.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we have a mapping diagram that \"reverses\" the action of the function $f.$ The diagram itself represents a new function, which we call the **inverse of the function $f,$** and denote by $f^{-1}.$\n\nWe can evaluate the inverse function $f^{-1}$ at any point in the range of $f.$ For example, to find the value of $f^{-1}(2),$ we notice that we have an arrow from ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right) to ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left). Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}({\\color{black}2}) = {\\color{black}0}.\n$$\n\nIn the original function, $f,$ that same arrow went from ${\\color{black}0}$ (on the left) to ${\\color{black}2}$ (on the right). So, we have that $f({\\color{black}0})={\\color{black}2},$  and as a result, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(0)) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, applying the function $f$ and then \"reversing\" the action using $f^{-1}$ will get us back to $0.$\n\nIn general, this is true for every $x$ in the domain of the function $f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(f(x)) = x\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084500 :tutorial/content "Recall that the ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) \"reverses\" the action of ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) so that\n![](images/ea/eab0458598a0b4298ccbfcd1664babf085309c0017ba0e530c76900f38bb8678.svg)\nwhich can be visualized using the mapping diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3dd33f3f90eaadec2f1168db89665d1986e562005fc01cd9f58dbc3f17e7a616.png)\n\n \n\nSince ![](images/aa/aabc5ff96d4807ff058c8f3e9f78aa70ced5e624ac2ae1ebdd1bb4597d6c6dad.svg) is just the composition ![](images/5a/5aeac36928f2e7a97b7c71c2c97dd9bef3774970b3efc871c9131c6d2075be5e.svg) we can now give the algebraic definition of the inverse. \n\nThe function ![](images/c5/c594a328984a211291c3547317baeb153ed97e2db7f43ab25ce204c31fe1796c.svg) is called the **inverse of the function ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg)** if\n![](images/52/52a4e32d6165988fe50c0766a1310fd3afb895f4a11149ff7b45c1cb0dd76e3a.svg)\nfor each ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) in the domain of ![](images/a0/a047a10a3ed3f5e86676e8fca21093abfe6a1849d90ce94b62101f34fca37e3d.svg)\n\nIn other words, to show that two functions ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) and ![](images/a9/a9e111e418e432fd154a819bb521c373ca64e9b27bbeed17a5777bba6940c6b6.svg) are inverses of each other, we need to check the following condition:\n![](images/7f/7facf5a55f1e90f9b9aac2358e4bc4b0560c2bd4b9fa2700e902a4dde84f90b7.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083443 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" "question-e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" :question/id #uuid "d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335606" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.5x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+4xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+4xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+4x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.5x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)(1-0.5(1))-2(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,0.75).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e096f83-a268-52b6-a0de-9b2e3f7fae60" "answer-field-2bb8730e-04ca-5e0f-9b3a-6b1eb07a3311" "answer-field-3420ffc8-c03a-5ee6-b400-0d64013fb521"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e096f83-a268-52b6-a0de-9b2e3f7fae60" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e096f83-a268-52b6-a0de-9b2e3f7fae60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-93049ccf-2b3b-5ef9-a326-d4f971a93e70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-93049ccf-2b3b-5ef9-a326-d4f971a93e70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93049ccf-2b3b-5ef9-a326-d4f971a93e70" :answer/id #uuid "93049ccf-2b3b-5ef9-a326-d4f971a93e70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2bb8730e-04ca-5e0f-9b3a-6b1eb07a3311" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb8730e-04ca-5e0f-9b3a-6b1eb07a3311" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a76d935c-2199-5460-a09a-2ef79825d59f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a76d935c-2199-5460-a09a-2ef79825d59f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a76d935c-2199-5460-a09a-2ef79825d59f" :answer/id #uuid "a76d935c-2199-5460-a09a-2ef79825d59f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3420ffc8-c03a-5ee6-b400-0d64013fb521" :answer-field/id #uuid "3420ffc8-c03a-5ee6-b400-0d64013fb521" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4a14350e-8f1d-5034-8c34-0c1be5812523" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a14350e-8f1d-5034-8c34-0c1be5812523"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a14350e-8f1d-5034-8c34-0c1be5812523" :answer/id #uuid "4a14350e-8f1d-5034-8c34-0c1be5812523" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e" :question/id #uuid "e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-335612" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.05x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.05x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.05x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(20,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.05(2))-3(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-6y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,0.6).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(20,0),\\,(2,0.6).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd1d7e76-9e39-5989-b9b2-3fd1ad45aa23" "answer-field-e4886f21-7c67-5733-a044-771bcc5c4cc0" "answer-field-2df6522f-2c6e-54e2-92a8-830be048fc64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd1d7e76-9e39-5989-b9b2-3fd1ad45aa23" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1d7e76-9e39-5989-b9b2-3fd1ad45aa23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4ca83931-fb18-5c7d-b857-e2bfb9743388" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ca83931-fb18-5c7d-b857-e2bfb9743388"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ca83931-fb18-5c7d-b857-e2bfb9743388" :answer/id #uuid "4ca83931-fb18-5c7d-b857-e2bfb9743388" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4886f21-7c67-5733-a044-771bcc5c4cc0" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4886f21-7c67-5733-a044-771bcc5c4cc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dd513e1b-1d09-5226-9b47-696ee3a40809" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd513e1b-1d09-5226-9b47-696ee3a40809"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd513e1b-1d09-5226-9b47-696ee3a40809" :answer/id #uuid "dd513e1b-1d09-5226-9b47-696ee3a40809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2df6522f-2c6e-54e2-92a8-830be048fc64" :answer-field/id #uuid "2df6522f-2c6e-54e2-92a8-830be048fc64" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5945fd09-32e0-5f46-bbe0-b89a233c1a71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5945fd09-32e0-5f46-bbe0-b89a233c1a71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5945fd09-32e0-5f46-bbe0-b89a233c1a71" :answer/id #uuid "5945fd09-32e0-5f46-bbe0-b89a233c1a71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e3d9b7ec-1851-512a-a201-72d69471f1e2" :activity/id #uuid "e3d9b7ec-1851-512a-a201-72d69471f1e2" :activity/title "The Lotka-Volterra Model With Carrying Capacity" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049289 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-2c4408e5-7391-59ee-b9e2-8bb13b7911cf" "step-2efaa86b-14b5-50a4-8011-f389ec1cb877" "step-77086ede-11b7-5f95-8032-15b20f19c4f2"] :activity/first-step "step-2c4408e5-7391-59ee-b9e2-8bb13b7911cf" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c4408e5-7391-59ee-b9e2-8bb13b7911cf" :step/id #uuid "2c4408e5-7391-59ee-b9e2-8bb13b7911cf" :step/content "question-d80de28b-ca2c-5fd8-ab88-370a0ca5902e" :step/next "step-2efaa86b-14b5-50a4-8011-f389ec1cb877" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2efaa86b-14b5-50a4-8011-f389ec1cb877" :step/id #uuid "2efaa86b-14b5-50a4-8011-f389ec1cb877" :step/content "question-e3f73f08-cefc-5b56-bacc-8fec801a6a5e" :step/next "step-77086ede-11b7-5f95-8032-15b20f19c4f2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-77086ede-11b7-5f95-8032-15b20f19c4f2" :step/id #uuid "77086ede-11b7-5f95-8032-15b20f19c4f2" :step/content 17592186370423 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186082511 :answer-field/choices 17592186082512] [:db/retract 17592186082511 :answer-field/choices 17592186082513] [:db/retract 17592186082511 :answer-field/choices 17592186082514] [:db/retract 17592186082511 :answer-field/choices 17592186082515] [:db/retract 17592186082511 :answer-field/choices 17592186082516] [:db/retract 17592186081863 :answer-field/choices 17592186081864] [:db/retract 17592186081863 :answer-field/choices 17592186081865] [:db/retract 17592186081863 :answer-field/choices 17592186081866] [:db/retract 17592186081863 :answer-field/choices 17592186081867] [:db/retract 17592186081863 :answer-field/choices 17592186081868] [:db/retract 17592186080667 :answer-field/choices 17592186080672] [:db/retract 17592186080667 :answer-field/choices 17592186080668] [:db/retract 17592186080667 :answer-field/choices 17592186080669] [:db/retract 17592186080667 :answer-field/choices 17592186080670] [:db/retract 17592186080667 :answer-field/choices 17592186080671] {:db/id 17592186083004 :question/problem "Use synthetic division to divide $5x^4+x^3+6x+2$ by $x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing an $x^2$ term, so we insert a $0x^2$ in its place and carry out the process in the same way.\n\n![](images/46/469e53d0e8ca69548d5385e8a4f75c3c366c59ce0c48b7eccf71f6bdb5577fcc.svg)\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ it means that $x+1$ divides $5x^4+x^3+6x+2$ exactly and gives a quotient of $5x^3-4x^2+4x+2.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x^4+x^3+6x+2}{x+1} = 5x^3-4x^2+4x+2 .\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083204 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-74edae7f-921c-52ab-b265-0f5c05fcd5a7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083005 :question/problem "Find the quotient and remainder of $\\dfrac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{2-3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the divisor in standard form as $-3x+2.$ Then, we factor out the leading coefficient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{-3x+2} & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{-3(x-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\dfrac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)},$ remembering to include a coefficient of zero for the $x^2$ term, we arrive at the following:\n\n![](images/7a/7a44598088833fa475191b39579bb82935074ae5c470e5a2139555818b91af08.svg)\n\nReading off the bottom row, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x^4+7x^3-10x+8}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)} = 3x^3+9x^2+6x-6+\\frac{4}{\\left(x-\\frac23\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by the original $-\\dfrac 1 3$ factor to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-10x+8}{2-3x} & =-\\frac{1}{3}(3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+6x-6+\\frac{4}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-2x+2+\\frac{4}{2-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083930 :question/problem "$\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-4}{x-3}=2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082511 :answer-field/correct "answer-4a1ad295-850f-513c-968f-e870eb960421" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a089de6a-9ae9-59ae-bc4b-65be3819ab12" "answer-4a1ad295-850f-513c-968f-e870eb960421" "answer-000f20c9-eed8-54f6-abc2-91ab1c18f546" "answer-3187e3c7-0514-556c-842f-9facdb881681" "answer-f94cbbcb-f375-5b97-b880-c565c568eb32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a089de6a-9ae9-59ae-bc4b-65be3819ab12" :answer/id #uuid "a089de6a-9ae9-59ae-bc4b-65be3819ab12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+1+\\frac{5}{x-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a1ad295-850f-513c-968f-e870eb960421" :answer/id #uuid "4a1ad295-850f-513c-968f-e870eb960421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+3+\\frac{5}{x-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-000f20c9-eed8-54f6-abc2-91ab1c18f546" :answer/id #uuid "000f20c9-eed8-54f6-abc2-91ab1c18f546" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+5+\\frac{5}{x-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3187e3c7-0514-556c-842f-9facdb881681" :answer/id #uuid "3187e3c7-0514-556c-842f-9facdb881681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x+6+\\frac{5}{x-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f94cbbcb-f375-5b97-b880-c565c568eb32" :answer/id #uuid "f94cbbcb-f375-5b97-b880-c565c568eb32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-6-\\frac{5}{x-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083822 :question/problem "$\\frac{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+1}{x-5}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+1}{x-5}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $4$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $20$ | $110$ | $545$ |\n|  | $4$ | $22$ | $109$ | $546$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x+1}{x-5}=4{x}^{2}+22x+109+\\frac{546}{x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186081863 :answer-field/correct "answer-a35b14bc-ccb3-5c93-b237-5c5f1e489e5b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a35b14bc-ccb3-5c93-b237-5c5f1e489e5b" "answer-c7caa57e-8215-5335-a5e5-24c26f1cc179" "answer-fc85e694-f575-5c45-afd7-78e57139337f" "answer-76ba2d94-4d0f-5625-b4fa-7c143eff88d0" "answer-ad57b36e-4935-5ec1-a05f-5c4149783a90"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a35b14bc-ccb3-5c93-b237-5c5f1e489e5b" :answer/id #uuid "a35b14bc-ccb3-5c93-b237-5c5f1e489e5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+22x+109+\\frac{546}{x-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7caa57e-8215-5335-a5e5-24c26f1cc179" :answer/id #uuid "c7caa57e-8215-5335-a5e5-24c26f1cc179" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+22x+126+\\frac{546}{x-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc85e694-f575-5c45-afd7-78e57139337f" :answer/id #uuid "fc85e694-f575-5c45-afd7-78e57139337f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+22x+148+\\frac{356}{x-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76ba2d94-4d0f-5625-b4fa-7c143eff88d0" :answer/id #uuid "76ba2d94-4d0f-5625-b4fa-7c143eff88d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+31x+109+\\frac{546}{x-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad57b36e-4935-5ec1-a05f-5c4149783a90" :answer/id #uuid "ad57b36e-4935-5ec1-a05f-5c4149783a90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-18x-87+\\frac{316}{x-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74edae7f-921c-52ab-b265-0f5c05fcd5a7" :question/id #uuid "74edae7f-921c-52ab-b265-0f5c05fcd5a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-18648" :question/problem "$\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1} & =\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 8 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\  & ↓ & -4 & 0 & 1 \\\\  & 8 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{2}(8{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-2x-1}{2x+1}=4{x}^{2}-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31effb53-6d38-5d2b-a024-6dd5298f9ade"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31effb53-6d38-5d2b-a024-6dd5298f9ade" :answer-field/id #uuid "31effb53-6d38-5d2b-a024-6dd5298f9ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-28481e03-c1c4-5f3e-83d7-ab1fbae85a71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-53c86c1b-3036-55c5-85ff-2b7cf6a4cd21" "answer-b9080672-7e99-5db6-a47d-8bd2cd68cf73" "answer-28481e03-c1c4-5f3e-83d7-ab1fbae85a71" "answer-e2b31f76-5489-52c8-bea0-fceb9cc6bd92" "answer-77fb4b5e-897c-5ba2-93cd-e5ced7af3f1c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53c86c1b-3036-55c5-85ff-2b7cf6a4cd21" :answer/id #uuid "53c86c1b-3036-55c5-85ff-2b7cf6a4cd21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9080672-7e99-5db6-a47d-8bd2cd68cf73" :answer/id #uuid "b9080672-7e99-5db6-a47d-8bd2cd68cf73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28481e03-c1c4-5f3e-83d7-ab1fbae85a71" :answer/id #uuid "28481e03-c1c4-5f3e-83d7-ab1fbae85a71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2b31f76-5489-52c8-bea0-fceb9cc6bd92" :answer/id #uuid "e2b31f76-5489-52c8-bea0-fceb9cc6bd92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-2-\\frac{3}{2x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77fb4b5e-897c-5ba2-93cd-e5ced7af3f1c" :answer/id #uuid "77fb4b5e-897c-5ba2-93cd-e5ced7af3f1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{x}^{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083597 :question/problem "$\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can calculate $\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1} & =\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6(x-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $18$ | $27$ | $0$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n|  | $18$ | $30$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})} & =\\frac{1}{6}(18x+30+\\frac{5}{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}) \\\\  & =3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{18{x}^{2}+27x}{6x-1}=3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080667 :answer-field/correct "answer-f9cf598d-2125-51cc-895b-f9c3da066eb3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6a06afe-f8a5-5241-8ff2-96045c897fa9" "answer-7bc3a8d8-690d-59bb-86ec-c9b3a4aaa664" "answer-f9cf598d-2125-51cc-895b-f9c3da066eb3" "answer-019b887d-bf4a-554b-963c-0cfc346b873b" "answer-142e1cea-140f-5872-936a-5a1bb03e406e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6a06afe-f8a5-5241-8ff2-96045c897fa9" :answer/id #uuid "b6a06afe-f8a5-5241-8ff2-96045c897fa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+1+\\frac{5}{6x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bc3a8d8-690d-59bb-86ec-c9b3a4aaa664" :answer/id #uuid "7bc3a8d8-690d-59bb-86ec-c9b3a4aaa664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+2+\\frac{5}{6x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9cf598d-2125-51cc-895b-f9c3da066eb3" :answer/id #uuid "f9cf598d-2125-51cc-895b-f9c3da066eb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+5+\\frac{5}{6x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-019b887d-bf4a-554b-963c-0cfc346b873b" :answer/id #uuid "019b887d-bf4a-554b-963c-0cfc346b873b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-1+\\frac{5}{6x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-142e1cea-140f-5872-936a-5a1bb03e406e" :answer/id #uuid "142e1cea-140f-5872-936a-5a1bb03e406e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-\\frac{5}{6x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084466 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to divide two polynomials, such as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's get familiar with some terminology:\n\n- The ** dividend** is the polynomial that we want to divide, so $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ in this case.\n\n- The **divisor** is the polynomial that we want to divide the dividend by, so $x-3$ in this case.\n\n- The result of the division, which will be a polynomial, is called the **quotient**.\n\n- The **remainder** is what is left after the division.\n\nBefore we start, we need to check that the polynomials are written in standard form. In our case, the terms in both the numerator and denominator are in descending order of their degree, so we're ready to go!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084439 :tutorial/content "Our goal is to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use **synthetic division**. Synthetic division allows us to divide a polynomial by a factor of the form $(x-c).$ Here's how we do it.\n\n| ![](images/86/8657b84c4cee9d122c5ec7fb62e652133c02b433e9bb73fb690573dd29857fe6.svg) | Start off by writing the term $c$ of the divisor on the left side. Here, $c = {\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, write the coefficients of all terms of the dividend on the right side in descending order of their degree. Remember the sign! Also, if a term is missing, use $0$ as the coefficient. |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/89/89ff1cd5f573ae387a716a8a446cd0867dfd464df0d9d9d91d4e9fd1f17f31ca.svg) | Bring down the leading term of the dividend. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{red}2}.$ |\n| ![](images/80/80e8e2f6092444058ec459f28518d0bd66b47858b9605562f8041aa09acda45e.svg) | Multiply the leading term $\\color{red}2$ by ${\\color{blue}3 }.$ This gives ${{\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot \\color{red}{2}} = {\\color{green}6}.$ Place the result below the next term. |\n| ![](images/a4/a44903e5fb107c1737c9ca1a6d0ba869784c7443b81068591bdb4ac0f7489555.svg) | Add the last result ($\\color{green}{6}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-5}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-5}} + {\\color{green}{6}}={\\color{red}{1}}.$ |\n| ![](images/a6/a62d39bda1c2e3463ec07179055c47ac775369e9c0065725b9b607f70f56b68b.svg) | We now repeat the process. Multiply the last result ($\\color{red}1$ here) by $\\color{blue} 3$ and place the result below the next term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}= {\\color{green}{3}}.$ |\n| ![](images/73/73578072793a196046848a135a745d97d17a711987919e6cc8bb96cf9c9712e0.svg) | Now add the last result ($\\color{green}{3}$) to the term located above ($\\color{magenta}{-7}$) and write their sum below the line. This gives ${\\color{magenta}{-7}} + {\\color{green}{3}}={\\color{red}{-4}}.$ |\n| ![](images/7f/7fc91ddc948d8471c21fc38f7a4aca73204ca7ded9f5e867ae1dd2aa2dfde1f3.svg) | There is one last step. Again, multiply $\\color{blue} 3$ by the last result $\\color{red}-4$ and write it below the constant term. This gives ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\cdot{\\color{red}{(-4)}}={\\color{green}-12}.$ Finally, add $\\color{green}-12$ to $\\color{magenta}11$ to get ${\\color{blue}{-1}},$ which is the remainder. The remainder $\\color{blue}-1$ is important, so we put a box around it. |\n\nThe numbers $2,$ $1$ and $-4$ in the bottom row are the coefficients of the **quotient**. So from left to right, the quotient is\n\n$$\n2x^2+1x-4.\n$$\n\nThe **remainder** is the value in the box, so ${\\color{blue}-1}$ in this case. \n\nFinally, we can write the full result of the division as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4+\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-1}{x-3} ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084483 :tutorial/content "Dividing $2x^3-5x^2-7x+11$ by $x-3,$ we obtained that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}{x-3}=2x^2+x-4-\\dfrac{1}{x-3}.\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides by $x-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2x^3-5x^2-7x+11}=(x-3)(2x^2+x-4)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to check if we have done the division correctly, we can simply multiply the polynomials on the right-hand side and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-1 & =x(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-3(2{x}^{2}+x-4)-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-4x-6{x}^{2}-3x+12-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-7x+11\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084440 :tutorial/content "We have already seen how synthetic division can be used to quickly divide a polynomial by a linear binomial with a leading coefficient of $1.$ \n\nIn fact, synthetic division can be used when the divisor is *any* linear binomial, like $(ax+b).$ We just factor out the leading coefficient $a,$ carry out the division in the standard way, and then include the leading coefficient in the final result.\n\nLet's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083423 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064379 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" "question-d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" :question/id #uuid "0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-2555" :question/problem "![](images/89/8946fc7f3b9a087304d893d69196f8462913974aab552b05a442a7460a619bef.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{2}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e8c5f3c7-fd49-5b7c-9172-09702c7f16cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8c5f3c7-fd49-5b7c-9172-09702c7f16cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c5f3c7-fd49-5b7c-9172-09702c7f16cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9aa873a6-fa87-5112-b328-59b790bb02cc" "answer-a28cf962-96ea-5fd2-a648-e08c58efd301" "answer-a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" "answer-ec3d86cc-339a-5c68-b539-6749321e41e0" "answer-a75bdbec-56f0-53b5-95b6-f2d3c36945c4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9aa873a6-fa87-5112-b328-59b790bb02cc" :answer/id #uuid "9aa873a6-fa87-5112-b328-59b790bb02cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a28cf962-96ea-5fd2-a648-e08c58efd301" :answer/id #uuid "a28cf962-96ea-5fd2-a648-e08c58efd301" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/id #uuid "a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec3d86cc-339a-5c68-b539-6749321e41e0" :answer/id #uuid "ec3d86cc-339a-5c68-b539-6749321e41e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a75bdbec-56f0-53b5-95b6-f2d3c36945c4" :answer/id #uuid "a75bdbec-56f0-53b5-95b6-f2d3c36945c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186296734 :answer-field/correct "answer-8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe" :answer/id #uuid "8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" :question/id #uuid "d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" :question/math-academy-id "q-344736" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc7e248783ccf6b1eda95a1c2c80300e48d8cdb48f39ea07296ed3bf51bfaa29.png)\n\nA small, circular puddle evaporates on a floor. If the puddle’s area is decreasing at a rate of $32\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ calculate the rate of change of its circumference at the moment when the radius is $8\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe circumference of the puddle is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=8,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=8\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (8)=16\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi $ and $C=16\\pi ,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{16\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2c7ad603-536b-5d70-82a1-a923240f239d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2c7ad603-536b-5d70-82a1-a923240f239d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7ad603-536b-5d70-82a1-a923240f239d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/id #uuid "db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" :question/id #uuid "7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" :question/math-academy-id "q-344787" :question/problem "A spherical hailstone is growing as it moves through a storm cloud. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the hailstone is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the hailstone at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & =(12\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e74b5676-bbc7-5018-8226-6434bdbb2081"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e74b5676-bbc7-5018-8226-6434bdbb2081" :answer-field/id #uuid "e74b5676-bbc7-5018-8226-6434bdbb2081" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/id #uuid "ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d4e9f94b-d25c-5269-b052-607fcefd509e" :activity/id #uuid "d4e9f94b-d25c-5269-b052-607fcefd509e" :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050411 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-5dd093f9-e085-5318-9537-a6bfe287bc53" "step-32bc3605-05ce-5789-aabb-0e63516ca447" "step-98f6e106-e4e9-50d2-919d-6bd1471cddd5" "step-16587f29-126f-56b6-8d74-cf78a419ed2b" "step-6e4aa70b-d6b4-5bbf-8a4a-5884ae9c9244"] :activity/first-step "step-5dd093f9-e085-5318-9537-a6bfe287bc53" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-5dd093f9-e085-5318-9537-a6bfe287bc53" :step/id #uuid "5dd093f9-e085-5318-9537-a6bfe287bc53" :step/content "question-0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" :step/next "step-32bc3605-05ce-5789-aabb-0e63516ca447" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-32bc3605-05ce-5789-aabb-0e63516ca447" :step/id #uuid "32bc3605-05ce-5789-aabb-0e63516ca447" :step/content 17592186297434 :step/next "step-98f6e106-e4e9-50d2-919d-6bd1471cddd5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-98f6e106-e4e9-50d2-919d-6bd1471cddd5" :step/id #uuid "98f6e106-e4e9-50d2-919d-6bd1471cddd5" :step/content "question-d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" :step/next "step-16587f29-126f-56b6-8d74-cf78a419ed2b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-16587f29-126f-56b6-8d74-cf78a419ed2b" :step/id #uuid "16587f29-126f-56b6-8d74-cf78a419ed2b" :step/content 17592186317539 :step/next "step-6e4aa70b-d6b4-5bbf-8a4a-5884ae9c9244" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6e4aa70b-d6b4-5bbf-8a4a-5884ae9c9244" :step/id #uuid "6e4aa70b-d6b4-5bbf-8a4a-5884ae9c9244" :step/content "question-7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172138 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-39734cdd-98b1-5bc5-a3ba-0e209592784a" "question-b1cfb6c9-bb07-5ff5-8e41-4388a89b7e12"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-39734cdd-98b1-5bc5-a3ba-0e209592784a" :question/id #uuid "39734cdd-98b1-5bc5-a3ba-0e209592784a" :question/math-academy-id "q-13411" :question/problem "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is $(-5,4)$ and leading coefficient is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,4)$ and $a=2$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(x-(-5))^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2(x+5)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+10x+25)+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+20x+50+4 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+20x+54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y=2{x}^{2}+20x+54.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d3f167d-1c36-5660-a850-11a37b74e56b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d3f167d-1c36-5660-a850-11a37b74e56b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d3f167d-1c36-5660-a850-11a37b74e56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7e7dbffc-f213-5b47-90bd-daf4ab1c319a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2359fbce-83d6-54b9-89ea-c9e3b3715a00" "answer-0f860513-b831-5135-a97b-6bb00ddca02d" "answer-0cd5a798-2c08-573f-b6a3-6bcc3b671500" "answer-cefc0cc3-384d-5cca-acd1-5118bce61b79" "answer-7e7dbffc-f213-5b47-90bd-daf4ab1c319a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2359fbce-83d6-54b9-89ea-c9e3b3715a00" :answer/id #uuid "2359fbce-83d6-54b9-89ea-c9e3b3715a00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+10x+36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f860513-b831-5135-a97b-6bb00ddca02d" :answer/id #uuid "0f860513-b831-5135-a97b-6bb00ddca02d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+12x+18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cd5a798-2c08-573f-b6a3-6bcc3b671500" :answer/id #uuid "0cd5a798-2c08-573f-b6a3-6bcc3b671500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+12x+6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cefc0cc3-384d-5cca-acd1-5118bce61b79" :answer/id #uuid "cefc0cc3-384d-5cca-acd1-5118bce61b79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+16x+9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e7dbffc-f213-5b47-90bd-daf4ab1c319a" :answer/id #uuid "7e7dbffc-f213-5b47-90bd-daf4ab1c319a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}+20x+54" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172137 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1b44bc96-863e-5a73-a2ed-628d5188e295"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1b44bc96-863e-5a73-a2ed-628d5188e295" :question/id #uuid "1b44bc96-863e-5a73-a2ed-628d5188e295" :question/math-academy-id "q-53700" :question/problem "Write the equation of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}-8x-14$ in vertex form." :question/worked-solution "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square. \n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}-8x-14=-2({x}^{2}+4x+7)\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+7 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+7 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot x+{2}^{2})-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4+7 & = \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+4x+7) & = \\\\ -2((x+2)^{2}+3) & = \\\\ -2(x+2)^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is $y=-2(x+2)^{2}-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be2e5806-e4f1-5967-90f4-2ffdb0fd7652"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be2e5806-e4f1-5967-90f4-2ffdb0fd7652" :answer-field/id #uuid "be2e5806-e4f1-5967-90f4-2ffdb0fd7652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-044741b5-1377-52d6-ad6f-88da086986f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1a97644-8ca9-5dc3-82d6-86d67ef9b796" "answer-530f0edf-0df9-5cdb-8936-b5d96998e8e9" "answer-044741b5-1377-52d6-ad6f-88da086986f6" "answer-fa1e6118-37dd-5e5e-9039-29e80ac12e98" "answer-2aa27a7f-66c9-5c3c-a1c6-570268e0230b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1a97644-8ca9-5dc3-82d6-86d67ef9b796" :answer/id #uuid "a1a97644-8ca9-5dc3-82d6-86d67ef9b796" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2(x+2)^{2}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-530f0edf-0df9-5cdb-8936-b5d96998e8e9" :answer/id #uuid "530f0edf-0df9-5cdb-8936-b5d96998e8e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2(x+2)^{2}-11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-044741b5-1377-52d6-ad6f-88da086986f6" :answer/id #uuid "044741b5-1377-52d6-ad6f-88da086986f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2(x+2)^{2}-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa1e6118-37dd-5e5e-9039-29e80ac12e98" :answer/id #uuid "fa1e6118-37dd-5e5e-9039-29e80ac12e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2(x-2)^{2}+7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aa27a7f-66c9-5c3c-a1c6-570268e0230b" :answer/id #uuid "2aa27a7f-66c9-5c3c-a1c6-570268e0230b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2(x-2)^{2}-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1cfb6c9-bb07-5ff5-8e41-4388a89b7e12" :question/id #uuid "b1cfb6c9-bb07-5ff5-8e41-4388a89b7e12" :question/math-academy-id "q-106267" :question/problem "Find the equation of the parabola whose vertex is at $(-8,-20)$ and whose leading coefficient is $1.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola, and $a$ is the leading coefficient.\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-20)$ and $a=1$ into the above and then expanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1(x-(-8))^{2}-20 \\\\  & =(x+8)^{2}-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+16x+64-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+16x+44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is $y={x}^{2}+16x+44.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89f78a2b-38d6-57b1-bd43-e097413569f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89f78a2b-38d6-57b1-bd43-e097413569f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "89f78a2b-38d6-57b1-bd43-e097413569f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7b334842-d776-5002-89d1-297f60f2ce75" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64f46c4f-ef14-508b-ba0b-1d70cbde1e44" "answer-03c5f552-e5b6-5559-a765-f234acd45481" "answer-cc9df875-09de-5c17-832a-6e4fe1c917ba" "answer-7b334842-d776-5002-89d1-297f60f2