#uuid "a0522211-b80f-5c0f-b2da-70dcd1257145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0c3261b6-3ec8-5138-960e-090e166d83ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fef18c21-70a3-5857-af0d-6f8d7ed21a10" "answer-c7e9e031-b3d5-5906-a23f-a324a3d38b44" "answer-1aabb5f6-195d-588b-b75e-6c1c6a8b2338" "answer-0c3261b6-3ec8-5138-960e-090e166d83ca" "answer-10fa8ad3-449b-5fca-9097-ecdf6c0d44a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fef18c21-70a3-5857-af0d-6f8d7ed21a10" :answer/id #uuid "fef18c21-70a3-5857-af0d-6f8d7ed21a10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.364" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7e9e031-b3d5-5906-a23f-a324a3d38b44" :answer/id #uuid "c7e9e031-b3d5-5906-a23f-a324a3d38b44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.464" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aabb5f6-195d-588b-b75e-6c1c6a8b2338" :answer/id #uuid "1aabb5f6-195d-588b-b75e-6c1c6a8b2338" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.643" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c3261b6-3ec8-5138-960e-090e166d83ca" :answer/id #uuid "0c3261b6-3ec8-5138-960e-090e166d83ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.644" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10fa8ad3-449b-5fca-9097-ecdf6c0d44a4" :answer/id #uuid "10fa8ad3-449b-5fca-9097-ecdf6c0d44a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.643" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074263 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186071816 :answer-field/correct 17592186071819 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-934b60d8-f889-5c91-8ad2-ede254ef4d64" :question/id #uuid "934b60d8-f889-5c91-8ad2-ede254ef4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-54418" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-716b3e70-e198-5303-8455-9f55371b225a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-716b3e70-e198-5303-8455-9f55371b225a" :answer-field/id #uuid "716b3e70-e198-5303-8455-9f55371b225a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c55ebeb-7cce-5cfe-953e-91fd1d9a3ec2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f3400f05-0efd-579c-9c4d-2bee0c52b9db" "answer-0ffe8b5a-89d1-5cc4-a628-30dfce5ca486" "answer-d4266c13-546a-5d43-a39f-e09844714267" "answer-1d6158b8-7fa1-5374-8102-b315d5ee7d9a" "answer-4c55ebeb-7cce-5cfe-953e-91fd1d9a3ec2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3400f05-0efd-579c-9c4d-2bee0c52b9db" :answer/id #uuid "f3400f05-0efd-579c-9c4d-2bee0c52b9db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ffe8b5a-89d1-5cc4-a628-30dfce5ca486" :answer/id #uuid "0ffe8b5a-89d1-5cc4-a628-30dfce5ca486" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4266c13-546a-5d43-a39f-e09844714267" :answer/id #uuid "d4266c13-546a-5d43-a39f-e09844714267" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d6158b8-7fa1-5374-8102-b315d5ee7d9a" :answer/id #uuid "1d6158b8-7fa1-5374-8102-b315d5ee7d9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c55ebeb-7cce-5cfe-953e-91fd1d9a3ec2" :answer/id #uuid "4c55ebeb-7cce-5cfe-953e-91fd1d9a3ec2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4cd34dd2-403c-55c2-9000-3c54447738a1" :question/id #uuid "4cd34dd2-403c-55c2-9000-3c54447738a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123545" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{57}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{60})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{35})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{14}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{80})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${57}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{57}^{∘}=57\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{60}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${32}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{32}^{∘}=32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{8\\pi }{35}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${14}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{14}^{∘}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{7\\pi }{80}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76bea3b5-fc60-57ec-bd8b-23889bb99a1f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76bea3b5-fc60-57ec-bd8b-23889bb99a1f" :answer-field/id #uuid "76bea3b5-fc60-57ec-bd8b-23889bb99a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24e8e760-7f22-552c-92c8-7d281f94ccea" "answer-2d802dae-c64e-582a-850a-c8d967eab5f8" "answer-3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" "answer-4d275836-192b-5d91-b4e5-fe1fa63b7154" "answer-3f23a7ae-da79-5087-902a-7b787f048816"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24e8e760-7f22-552c-92c8-7d281f94ccea" :answer/id #uuid "24e8e760-7f22-552c-92c8-7d281f94ccea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d802dae-c64e-582a-850a-c8d967eab5f8" :answer/id #uuid "2d802dae-c64e-582a-850a-c8d967eab5f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer/id #uuid "3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d275836-192b-5d91-b4e5-fe1fa63b7154" :answer/id #uuid "4d275836-192b-5d91-b4e5-fe1fa63b7154" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f23a7ae-da79-5087-902a-7b787f048816" :answer/id #uuid "3f23a7ae-da79-5087-902a-7b787f048816" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f7892f8a-0a7a-5c55-9033-6382c732bb3a" :question/id #uuid "f7892f8a-0a7a-5c55-9033-6382c732bb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123546" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{110}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{18})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{140}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{9})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{170}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{18})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${110}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{110}^{∘}=110\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${140}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{140}^{∘}=140\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${170}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{170}^{∘}=170\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1d98aaaa-2215-595e-9cfe-13f69f4453bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1d98aaaa-2215-595e-9cfe-13f69f4453bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d98aaaa-2215-595e-9cfe-13f69f4453bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d9f692f-ebf1-58e1-8b6f-d036750bb7c4" "answer-3ee69202-d794-5dc7-b9e1-48471ca549b4" "answer-f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" "answer-132bb860-1537-5d50-8e8d-eab1cc552fe4" "answer-0fbc408b-c121-5287-963f-06a800c703d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d9f692f-ebf1-58e1-8b6f-d036750bb7c4" :answer/id #uuid "8d9f692f-ebf1-58e1-8b6f-d036750bb7c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ee69202-d794-5dc7-b9e1-48471ca549b4" :answer/id #uuid "3ee69202-d794-5dc7-b9e1-48471ca549b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-132bb860-1537-5d50-8e8d-eab1cc552fe4" :answer/id #uuid "132bb860-1537-5d50-8e8d-eab1cc552fe4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fbc408b-c121-5287-963f-06a800c703d0" :answer/id #uuid "0fbc408b-c121-5287-963f-06a800c703d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-da03fb8c-a7ca-5696-a1f6-3635e0d03dad" :question/id #uuid "da03fb8c-a7ca-5696-a1f6-3635e0d03dad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123550" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{22}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{90})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{94}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{37\\pi }{90})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{76}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{25})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${22}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{22}^{∘}=22\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${94}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{94}^{∘}=94\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{37\\pi }{90}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${76}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{76}^{∘}=76\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{19\\pi }{25}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afac089e-3d46-5d44-b08d-1f8fc4927c12"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afac089e-3d46-5d44-b08d-1f8fc4927c12" :answer-field/id #uuid "afac089e-3d46-5d44-b08d-1f8fc4927c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-217fd35f-043a-51ea-bb88-98a6243cbd0b" "answer-37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" "answer-b460d5d1-82bb-564e-b699-500fb25f2615" "answer-36627598-707e-5fca-8a0d-d812713fd5ac" "answer-a3e8c220-fc4e-5e93-9ea0-f85a2d09e7b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-217fd35f-043a-51ea-bb88-98a6243cbd0b" :answer/id #uuid "217fd35f-043a-51ea-bb88-98a6243cbd0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer/id #uuid "37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b460d5d1-82bb-564e-b699-500fb25f2615" :answer/id #uuid "b460d5d1-82bb-564e-b699-500fb25f2615" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36627598-707e-5fca-8a0d-d812713fd5ac" :answer/id #uuid "36627598-707e-5fca-8a0d-d812713fd5ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3e8c220-fc4e-5e93-9ea0-f85a2d09e7b6" :answer/id #uuid "a3e8c220-fc4e-5e93-9ea0-f85a2d09e7b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-299f3633-d5e0-546a-8fb6-eacb46c1b2dd" :question/id #uuid "299f3633-d5e0-546a-8fb6-eacb46c1b2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{9})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{50}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{80}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{9})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${50}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{50}^{∘}=50\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${80}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{80}^{∘}=80\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{2\\pi }{9}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bffaf27a-e722-5e41-898d-3903f71f347b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bffaf27a-e722-5e41-898d-3903f71f347b" :answer-field/id #uuid "bffaf27a-e722-5e41-898d-3903f71f347b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" "answer-a8691977-29a4-53b2-bc0b-9e23c70a02aa" "answer-15aac7dc-8cd1-5607-ba90-095a24d98488" "answer-35f3791a-7cf7-5558-b05a-1693353bfa6e" "answer-ce511b28-cdc4-5fcb-a0b8-330fbc0beae4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer/id #uuid "3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8691977-29a4-53b2-bc0b-9e23c70a02aa" :answer/id #uuid "a8691977-29a4-53b2-bc0b-9e23c70a02aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15aac7dc-8cd1-5607-ba90-095a24d98488" :answer/id #uuid "15aac7dc-8cd1-5607-ba90-095a24d98488" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35f3791a-7cf7-5558-b05a-1693353bfa6e" :answer/id #uuid "35f3791a-7cf7-5558-b05a-1693353bfa6e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce511b28-cdc4-5fcb-a0b8-330fbc0beae4" :answer/id #uuid "ce511b28-cdc4-5fcb-a0b8-330fbc0beae4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5168a992-4557-5020-911a-67c9df9985ed" :question/id #uuid "5168a992-4557-5020-911a-67c9df9985ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-123537" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{15}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{36})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{35}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{36})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${15}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{15}^{∘}=15\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{36}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${35}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{35}^{∘}=35\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{36}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and III only'." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1af2aa0f-c2a3-543f-a83e-691c13f6818c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1af2aa0f-c2a3-543f-a83e-691c13f6818c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1af2aa0f-c2a3-543f-a83e-691c13f6818c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c589619a-7081-5009-a45d-0f03134703e1" "answer-58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" "answer-20b415d0-ce3e-5252-babf-70efd7ce0657" "answer-78a7f04a-4b10-5b42-9c84-6a290d024e23" "answer-d1368367-9652-53bc-8d18-404303318464"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c589619a-7081-5009-a45d-0f03134703e1" :answer/id #uuid "c589619a-7081-5009-a45d-0f03134703e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer/id #uuid "58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20b415d0-ce3e-5252-babf-70efd7ce0657" :answer/id #uuid "20b415d0-ce3e-5252-babf-70efd7ce0657" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78a7f04a-4b10-5b42-9c84-6a290d024e23" :answer/id #uuid "78a7f04a-4b10-5b42-9c84-6a290d024e23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1368367-9652-53bc-8d18-404303318464" :answer/id #uuid "d1368367-9652-53bc-8d18-404303318464" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074993 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to apply trigonometric ratios to angles given in degrees, but we can also apply them to angles given in radians.\n\nFor example, let's consider the expression \n\n$$\n\\cos({\\color{red}1}).\n$$\n\nNote that $\\color{red}1$ is written without any special symbols, like ${}^\\circ$ (degrees). This indicates that the angle $\\color{red}1$ is given in *radians*. Here, $\\color{red}1$ reads as \"one radian.\"\n\nIf we were given $\\cos({\\color{blue}1^\\circ})$ instead, with the degree sign ${}^\\circ$, that would mean we want the cosine of \"one degree.\"\n\nThe simplest way to find the actual value of $\\cos(1)$ would be using a calculator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\cos(1) = 0.540\\,302\\ldots \\approx 0.540\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Watch out!** Don't forget to set your calculator into the \"radians\" mode before computing the trigonometric function above." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075001 :tutorial/content "Another way to compute a trigonometric ratio of an angle, say $\\sin\\theta$ where $\\theta$ is measured in radians, is to convert the angle into degrees first and then compute the trigonometric ratio of the equivalent angle in degrees.\n\nLet's consider the value of $\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}\\right).$ Since the angle is a multiple of $\\pi,$ we can easily convert the measure in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{10}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \\dfrac {180^\\circ} {10} = 18^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNow, using a calculator in \"degrees\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({18}^{∘}) \\\\  & =0.309\\,016… \\\\  & ≈0.309\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073941 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e8924afe-1a47-5aee-af84-25e72799ddd7" :activity/id #uuid "e8924afe-1a47-5aee-af84-25e72799ddd7" :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049676 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-4e9223b8-eead-5430-8672-05f220aed1df" "step-5c8783e4-94f5-56ac-a41a-69d2ba5ab312" "step-6003b5aa-8454-5323-bd58-1be79af915b0" "step-d17e39b1-6565-5f9d-b8bf-8cebdb011f05" "step-5b4f1eb6-06aa-5151-a935-a33c43790429"] :activity/first-step "step-4e9223b8-eead-5430-8672-05f220aed1df" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4e9223b8-eead-5430-8672-05f220aed1df" :step/id #uuid "4e9223b8-eead-5430-8672-05f220aed1df" :step/content 17592186284417 :step/next "step-5c8783e4-94f5-56ac-a41a-69d2ba5ab312" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5c8783e4-94f5-56ac-a41a-69d2ba5ab312" :step/id #uuid "5c8783e4-94f5-56ac-a41a-69d2ba5ab312" :step/content 17592186434760 :step/next "step-6003b5aa-8454-5323-bd58-1be79af915b0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6003b5aa-8454-5323-bd58-1be79af915b0" :step/id #uuid "6003b5aa-8454-5323-bd58-1be79af915b0" :step/content 17592186434752 :step/next "step-d17e39b1-6565-5f9d-b8bf-8cebdb011f05" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d17e39b1-6565-5f9d-b8bf-8cebdb011f05" :step/id #uuid "d17e39b1-6565-5f9d-b8bf-8cebdb011f05" :step/content 17592186433379 :step/next "step-5b4f1eb6-06aa-5151-a935-a33c43790429" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5b4f1eb6-06aa-5151-a935-a33c43790429" :step/id #uuid "5b4f1eb6-06aa-5151-a935-a33c43790429" :step/content 17592186434763 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186080933 :answer-field/choices 17592186080935] [:db/retract 17592186080933 :answer-field/choices 17592186080937] [:db/retract 17592186080933 :answer-field/choices 17592186080938] {:db/id 17592186083161 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8dc02ab1-2232-53a5-8f5c-8a9183793c53"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082978 :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse $f^{-1}(x),$ and the point $(2,9)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y=f^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y = f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y = f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y = f^{-1}(x).$ In other words, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\nSince the point $(2,9)$ lies on $y = f(x),$ the point $(9,2)$ lies on $y = f^{-1}(x).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083160 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-56462713-a3e2-5f05-8120-8de6685511d4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082977 :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Sketch  the graph of $y = f^{-1}(x).$\n\n![](images/99/99f02265f84874638b3faae106bb293e238b5e5064ea09beb2a6d71aeff12fe5.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that each point $(a,b)$ on $y = f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y = f^{-1}(x).$ In other words, the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y=f^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,1) & ⟶(1,0) \\\\ (-2,5) & ⟶(5,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo graph $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we plot the two points $(1,0)$ and $(5,-2)$ and connect the segment. We see that, as expected, the function $y = f^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y = f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/e7/e701f7e4f82b2ae3f6e48abcbbce350f57d6f54f9d4a98c03eabeaec89a328dd.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8dc02ab1-2232-53a5-8f5c-8a9183793c53" :question/id #uuid "8dc02ab1-2232-53a5-8f5c-8a9183793c53" :question/math-academy-id "q-66616" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(5,1)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(5,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(1,5)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e97ea637-210f-534c-9a36-7028bbfa41bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e97ea637-210f-534c-9a36-7028bbfa41bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "e97ea637-210f-534c-9a36-7028bbfa41bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd4d1f48-5be7-5ed8-887e-1f8ca7d1ca99" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83e5ec26-38cf-5113-82fa-51f609535188" "answer-336e2f7d-fd40-50a7-8a7b-30a742e79f73" "answer-8b01730a-325b-5954-acee-5baa39308c77" "answer-fc2a4f96-2c06-5aec-9e46-84e2bde15441" "answer-fd4d1f48-5be7-5ed8-887e-1f8ca7d1ca99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83e5ec26-38cf-5113-82fa-51f609535188" :answer/id #uuid "83e5ec26-38cf-5113-82fa-51f609535188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-336e2f7d-fd40-50a7-8a7b-30a742e79f73" :answer/id #uuid "336e2f7d-fd40-50a7-8a7b-30a742e79f73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b01730a-325b-5954-acee-5baa39308c77" :answer/id #uuid "8b01730a-325b-5954-acee-5baa39308c77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc2a4f96-2c06-5aec-9e46-84e2bde15441" :answer/id #uuid "fc2a4f96-2c06-5aec-9e46-84e2bde15441" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd4d1f48-5be7-5ed8-887e-1f8ca7d1ca99" :answer/id #uuid "fd4d1f48-5be7-5ed8-887e-1f8ca7d1ca99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083667 :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)$ has an inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$, and the point $(-1,-3)$ lies on the graph $y=f(x),$ which point must lie on the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Therefore, each point $(a,b)$ on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ (the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap).\n\nSince the point $(-1,-3)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ the point $(-3,-1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080933 :answer-field/correct 17592186080936 :answer-field/choices ["answer-5def6256-d0d4-5511-bf5c-30e0edd55937" "answer-7b9b2dea-96eb-5491-90c6-bbf646d32f55" "answer-3a648c59-715c-54b7-b0c3-e1bb681b4ef5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5def6256-d0d4-5511-bf5c-30e0edd55937" :answer/id #uuid "5def6256-d0d4-5511-bf5c-30e0edd55937" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b9b2dea-96eb-5491-90c6-bbf646d32f55" :answer/id #uuid "7b9b2dea-96eb-5491-90c6-bbf646d32f55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a648c59-715c-54b7-b0c3-e1bb681b4ef5" :answer/id #uuid "3a648c59-715c-54b7-b0c3-e1bb681b4ef5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56462713-a3e2-5f05-8120-8de6685511d4" :question/id #uuid "56462713-a3e2-5f05-8120-8de6685511d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-35203" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8788f03a4cb07d8f447346699ed5a9e8231cc4e496e135b6df1515e56792d61.png)\n\nThe graph of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is given above. Which of the following could be the graph of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/dd/ddf15053fee30ed69e2caed1a049b34c6f39c729fec0f1a053b00e33686c1e5f.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,6) & ⟶(6,0) \\\\ (1,4) & ⟶(4,1) \\\\ (2,-2) & ⟶(-2,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bb4cf861-b4f3-5455-8d12-03380d6287ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bb4cf861-b4f3-5455-8d12-03380d6287ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4cf861-b4f3-5455-8d12-03380d6287ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-693647bb-5f59-5558-9c9e-ca7a73a332f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-260f31b5-96b2-555d-83c9-1bb283302479" "answer-3f5b716d-a09a-50e9-bfb9-52fe8f8f9b62" "answer-693647bb-5f59-5558-9c9e-ca7a73a332f5" "answer-bd460502-1192-573f-8110-948e8967ad0d" "answer-c9ff6934-a2db-51c5-9ff6-47a57660d5df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-260f31b5-96b2-555d-83c9-1bb283302479" :answer/id #uuid "260f31b5-96b2-555d-83c9-1bb283302479" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/12/126802a67a98cdc4efbde202474440338a0d5bcfadc78c9f49c6593598c2dc95.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f5b716d-a09a-50e9-bfb9-52fe8f8f9b62" :answer/id #uuid "3f5b716d-a09a-50e9-bfb9-52fe8f8f9b62" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b4/b4f215c6aa44cfee48aaafb80de5dc3b471d6b40d0a422a6c19acbeaa5390d38.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-693647bb-5f59-5558-9c9e-ca7a73a332f5" :answer/id #uuid "693647bb-5f59-5558-9c9e-ca7a73a332f5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/de/de87c87d442144131163cf9b0044c348b168be9ac36d65e020618c97e58f545c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd460502-1192-573f-8110-948e8967ad0d" :answer/id #uuid "bd460502-1192-573f-8110-948e8967ad0d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f1/f12b2efb4eefc74f1238018a151028710bb05533599fde5719d4e965fe2c9138.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9ff6934-a2db-51c5-9ff6-47a57660d5df" :answer/id #uuid "c9ff6934-a2db-51c5-9ff6-47a57660d5df" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/fd/fd87d862944573906961220f61f7489a882877ebfdc71e0ff2501462c2eb986a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083666 :question/problem "![](images/46/46022df1ffb1b45ffe344658075dd363f3e2856798050fdbe85aacff0bc98ebe.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y={f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the line $y=x.$ Of the given options, the only graph satisfying this condition is the one shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53ba6c80f60bdf35052e0bc018317f741d4ddd781ed9156f915df91b855a93f7.png)\n\nNotice that the points on $y=f(x)$ have corresponding points on $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ with the coordinates swapped.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,1) & ⟶(1,0) \\\\ (-2,3) & ⟶(3,-2) \\\\ (-3,5) & ⟶(5,-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080927 :answer-field/correct 17592186080931 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084421 :tutorial/content "Consider the function \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f(x)} = 4x-2,\n$$\n\ndefined over the entire real line, and its inverse function \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}f^{-1}(x)} = \\dfrac{1}{4}(x+2).\n$$\n\nIf we plot both ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ and ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}$ on the same diagram, we can see that the graph of ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}$ is a **reflection** of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ in the line $y=x.$ \n\n![](images/ec/ec501e8c71fdcc1cfdd78e47ed81542bbba44aa8ac50084cc6ddfeb7eff011ff.png)\n\nEach point $(a,b)$ on the graph of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$ That is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\nFor example, notice that the point $A(2,6)$ on ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has the corresponding point $A'(6,2)$ on ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083446 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166967 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-30559f45-859e-514a-9084-78b866aea583" "question-2c6fa2f7-da40-5cad-be4c-16622f82a062" "question-77a3c45c-df66-5438-9ce6-8791cf4f16da" "question-b1a403c5-0788-5593-a233-1f2c8a521c2c" "question-1501d1dd-a30a-5622-a170-02307bfe9a8b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166577 :question/problem "Evaluate the indicator function\n$\\mathbf{1}[9 > 7].$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n![](images/b1/b1a45cb38a9a19c9dac7a7736cb71f2ad0cef5b7026f3eeb6bc573ee11609db4.svg)\n\nHere, the predicate is $9 > 7.$ Since $9$ is greater than $7,$ the predicate is true. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}[9 > 7] = 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166968 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ee0e20b3-c728-5a30-b95b-8f191603cec6" "question-e8d6c64e-e665-50e4-b8dc-09f085134af3" "question-45172067-6fd8-53b2-8b3e-3767eb3b99d5" "question-4946e8ca-eef4-5bca-ab77-2e318993cb3a" "question-d217e754-13bb-55c1-bf52-1609c0455dd0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166579 :question/problem "Let $H = \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $\\mathbf{1}_H(0)?$\n\nI. $\\mathbf{1}[0 \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{1}[0 \\notin \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{1}[H \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}]$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf{1}[\\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\} \\in 0]$\n\nV. $\\mathbf{1}[0 = H]$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n![](images/e9/e9658de5d9f87481ee638e6b3aaaa0d628cb4afd415aa73648d71ff3ddd6af00.svg)\n\nRecall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_H(x)$ of a set $H$ equals $1$ if $x \\in H$ and $0$ if $x \\notin H.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_H(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in H].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_H(0) = \\mathbf{1}[0 \\in H] = \\mathbf{1}[0 \\in \\{-5, -3, -1, 1\\}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166969 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e022df96-ab83-5f54-81a6-300069e7bfc3" "question-ce2c0db9-ad0b-5196-91d2-2f4a888aa062" "question-f9506abb-49a9-5357-8f1d-b8154670131b" "question-7ce60c02-eb25-536f-a376-c9d5685f21ea" "question-cde00f71-de2d-5ff7-ae71-1ddcfa0a1b1f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166578 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 1] \\cdot x^2 + \\mathbf{1}[x < 1] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $f(-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n![](images/66/6698e70e8d5cc4c4e4fd3578170ed7e43a2a907e2ab0397352882d87af2d32e1.svg)\n\nFor $x = -2,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 1] = \\mathbf{1}[-2 \\geq 1] = 0$ (since $-2 \\geq 1$ is false)\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[x < 1] = \\mathbf{1}[-2 < 1] = 1$ (since $-2 < 1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1[-2\\ge 1]\\cdot (-2)^{2}+1[-2<1]\\cdot (-(-2)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x = -2 < 1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-30559f45-859e-514a-9084-78b866aea583" :question/id #uuid "30559f45-859e-514a-9084-78b866aea583" :question/math-academy-id "q-346659" :question/problem "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}].$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}.$ Since $9$ is not an element of the set $\\{1,2,3,5\\},$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}]=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bfad7e9d-4e2c-535c-b3a5-b71dee44a203"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bfad7e9d-4e2c-535c-b3a5-b71dee44a203" :answer-field/id #uuid "bfad7e9d-4e2c-535c-b3a5-b71dee44a203" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a654912f-2a18-507b-a9d6-7aa55d6b5063" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a654912f-2a18-507b-a9d6-7aa55d6b5063" "answer-375e6424-d095-5ddd-9530-7065ad6f75a8" "answer-5d553bf8-8c0c-58a6-8637-fdd966562bbb" "answer-61f08d86-aa99-5e5f-83f4-65e893563765" "answer-ceb14721-f32c-59f1