ion as\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(x)=1[x\\in B].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(4)=1[4\\in B]=1[4\\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e157aa0-f544-5502-acc6-0625ed40b4c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e157aa0-f544-5502-acc6-0625ed40b4c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e157aa0-f544-5502-acc6-0625ed40b4c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-694ef196-0766-5926-a457-4750f88d8632" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f7a6f0e-6445-5701-a9d9-31d89e8a87a3" "answer-694ef196-0766-5926-a457-4750f88d8632" "answer-cbcdc340-fc9e-5215-9bcb-fb66512e975c" "answer-abd78e39-0094-50c0-bfb3-a65b9260681f" "answer-476fea80-a151-5a7d-8ed4-0f75973ec718"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f7a6f0e-6445-5701-a9d9-31d89e8a87a3" :answer/id #uuid "4f7a6f0e-6445-5701-a9d9-31d89e8a87a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[4=B]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-694ef196-0766-5926-a457-4750f88d8632" :answer/id #uuid "694ef196-0766-5926-a457-4750f88d8632" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[4\\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbcdc340-fc9e-5215-9bcb-fb66512e975c" :answer/id #uuid "cbcdc340-fc9e-5215-9bcb-fb66512e975c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[4\\notin \\{2,4,6,8\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abd78e39-0094-50c0-bfb3-a65b9260681f" :answer/id #uuid "abd78e39-0094-50c0-bfb3-a65b9260681f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[B\\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-476fea80-a151-5a7d-8ed4-0f75973ec718" :answer/id #uuid "476fea80-a151-5a7d-8ed4-0f75973ec718" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[\\{2,4,6,8\\}\\in 4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4946e8ca-eef4-5bca-ab77-2e318993cb3a" :question/id #uuid "4946e8ca-eef4-5bca-ab77-2e318993cb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346664" :question/problem "Let $E=\\{1,4,9\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{E}(9)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{E}(x)$ of a set $E$ equals $1$ if $x\\in E$ and $0$ if $x\\notin E.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(x)=1[x\\in E].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(9)=1[9\\in E]=1[9\\in \\{1,4,9\\}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae3cbd01-def2-5c98-9377-4568488434a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae3cbd01-def2-5c98-9377-4568488434a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae3cbd01-def2-5c98-9377-4568488434a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6a58bef4-93d4-522c-a87f-49921e906d27" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac497d10-6ba2-5055-b7db-ce4bc1ac8d86" "answer-6a58bef4-93d4-522c-a87f-49921e906d27" "answer-dd3caf27-76bf-5eb7-b4a6-c97e14006602" "answer-fd817330-5cc3-53c0-959a-052ddfc3d829" "answer-95e7e21b-2045-5591-bb93-0129146c3083"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac497d10-6ba2-5055-b7db-ce4bc1ac8d86" :answer/id #uuid "ac497d10-6ba2-5055-b7db-ce4bc1ac8d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[9=E]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a58bef4-93d4-522c-a87f-49921e906d27" :answer/id #uuid "6a58bef4-93d4-522c-a87f-49921e906d27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[9\\in \\{1,4,9\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd3caf27-76bf-5eb7-b4a6-c97e14006602" :answer/id #uuid "dd3caf27-76bf-5eb7-b4a6-c97e14006602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[9\\notin \\{1,4,9\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd817330-5cc3-53c0-959a-052ddfc3d829" :answer/id #uuid "fd817330-5cc3-53c0-959a-052ddfc3d829" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[E\\in \\{1,4,9\\}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e7e21b-2045-5591-bb93-0129146c3083" :answer/id #uuid "95e7e21b-2045-5591-bb93-0129146c3083" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1[\\{1,4,9\\}\\in 9]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d217e754-13bb-55c1-bf52-1609c0455dd0" :question/id #uuid "d217e754-13bb-55c1-bf52-1609c0455dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-351127" :question/problem "Let $C=\\{1,3,7,9\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[4\\in C]?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[4\\in C]$ has $x=4$ and $A=C.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[4\\in C]={1}_{C}(4).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-471ddc6c-eb77-5611-9970-f361d660ca90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-471ddc6c-eb77-5611-9970-f361d660ca90" :answer-field/id #uuid "471ddc6c-eb77-5611-9970-f361d660ca90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-72e680b9-d1e0-5ace-914f-85201a54e8c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a188e1ba-6c69-51b2-b51a-8bfb5787bc58" "answer-e8f566a8-2d46-514e-a5f1-e7a99bb09834" "answer-96328b68-99be-544f-acc5-512aa00ccca7" "answer-72e680b9-d1e0-5ace-914f-85201a54e8c8" "answer-01e2b004-c1e0-5ea3-8c85-931b530092a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a188e1ba-6c69-51b2-b51a-8bfb5787bc58" :answer/id #uuid "a188e1ba-6c69-51b2-b51a-8bfb5787bc58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{4}(1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8f566a8-2d46-514e-a5f1-e7a99bb09834" :answer/id #uuid "e8f566a8-2d46-514e-a5f1-e7a99bb09834" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{4}(C)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96328b68-99be-544f-acc5-512aa00ccca7" :answer/id #uuid "96328b68-99be-544f-acc5-512aa00ccca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{C}(3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72e680b9-d1e0-5ace-914f-85201a54e8c8" :answer/id #uuid "72e680b9-d1e0-5ace-914f-85201a54e8c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{C}(4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01e2b004-c1e0-5ea3-8c85-931b530092a0" :answer/id #uuid "01e2b004-c1e0-5ea3-8c85-931b530092a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{1}_{\\{1,3,7,9\\}}(C)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e022df96-ab83-5f54-81a6-300069e7bfc3" :question/id #uuid "e022df96-ab83-5f54-81a6-300069e7bfc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-346680" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=1[x\\ge -1]\\cdot {x}^{2}+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $f(-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge -1]=1[-3\\ge -1]=0$ (since $-3\\ge -1$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =1[-3\\ge -1]\\cdot (-3)^{2}+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1414c5ad-3d2c-5faf-add7-3444b17c5202"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1414c5ad-3d2c-5faf-add7-3444b17c5202" :answer-field/id #uuid "1414c5ad-3d2c-5faf-add7-3444b17c5202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21d85309-3a31-5b9c-abc2-4783d8a10ad3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0203717-1849-5686-90b7-0f7457dd17db" "answer-f617a0e6-381f-5f77-b895-286ad10d9598" "answer-601ff206-5a9b-55e2-8466-b8841840c228" "answer-21d85309-3a31-5b9c-abc2-4783d8a10ad3" "answer-4927cc7c-73e9-5c12-8f55-724aa84b4cf9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0203717-1849-5686-90b7-0f7457dd17db" :answer/id #uuid "e0203717-1849-5686-90b7-0f7457dd17db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f617a0e6-381f-5f77-b895-286ad10d9598" :answer/id #uuid "f617a0e6-381f-5f77-b895-286ad10d9598" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-601ff206-5a9b-55e2-8466-b8841840c228" :answer/id #uuid "601ff206-5a9b-55e2-8466-b8841840c228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21d85309-3a31-5b9c-abc2-4783d8a10ad3" :answer/id #uuid "21d85309-3a31-5b9c-abc2-4783d8a10ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4927cc7c-73e9-5c12-8f55-724aa84b4cf9" :answer/id #uuid "4927cc7c-73e9-5c12-8f55-724aa84b4cf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ce2c0db9-ad0b-5196-91d2-2f4a888aa062" :question/id #uuid "ce2c0db9-ad0b-5196-91d2-2f4a888aa062" :question/math-academy-id "q-346669" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=6,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=2,\\,{x}_{5}=-1\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[-3>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[6>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 6+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0+6+0+2+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51fa0505-47ca-5c35-ba62-02fa3db61539"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51fa0505-47ca-5c35-ba62-02fa3db61539" :answer-field/id #uuid "51fa0505-47ca-5c35-ba62-02fa3db61539" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-80a200ec-9e32-5842-a3a1-191293b65d99" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80a200ec-9e32-5842-a3a1-191293b65d99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80a200ec-9e32-5842-a3a1-191293b65d99" :answer/id #uuid "80a200ec-9e32-5842-a3a1-191293b65d99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9506abb-49a9-5357-8f1d-b8154670131b" :question/id #uuid "f9506abb-49a9-5357-8f1d-b8154670131b" :question/math-academy-id "q-346673" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=-7,\\,{x}_{3}=8,\\,{x}_{4}=9,\\,{x}_{5}=-2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-7>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[8>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-2>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot 8+1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0+0+8+9+0 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0394d37a-0a43-5dc1-accb-790c982f5ce9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0394d37a-0a43-5dc1-accb-790c982f5ce9" :answer-field/id #uuid "0394d37a-0a43-5dc1-accb-790c982f5ce9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1374ccaf-10b6-5249-9706-254726bc070c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1374ccaf-10b6-5249-9706-254726bc070c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1374ccaf-10b6-5249-9706-254726bc070c" :answer/id #uuid "1374ccaf-10b6-5249-9706-254726bc070c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ce60c02-eb25-536f-a376-c9d5685f21ea" :question/id #uuid "7ce60c02-eb25-536f-a376-c9d5685f21ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-347932" :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=6,\\,{x}_{4}=-2,\\,{x}_{5}=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2$ equals ${x}_{i}-1$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $2$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2\n$$\n\nadds ${x}_{i}-1$ for nonnegative values and $2$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-3\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[1\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[6\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[-2\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2 & =2+(1-1)+(6-1)+2+(0-1) \\\\  & =2+0+5+2-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8470a13f-71f6-57ad-8421-53498bc36c05"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8470a13f-71f6-57ad-8421-53498bc36c05" :answer-field/id #uuid "8470a13f-71f6-57ad-8421-53498bc36c05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-50fd8bc5-cefc-5845-bbed-5233a3393494" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50fd8bc5-cefc-5845-bbed-5233a3393494"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50fd8bc5-cefc-5845-bbed-5233a3393494" :answer/id #uuid "50fd8bc5-cefc-5845-bbed-5233a3393494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cde00f71-de2d-5ff7-ae71-1ddcfa0a1b1f" :question/id #uuid "cde00f71-de2d-5ff7-ae71-1ddcfa0a1b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-347433" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=1[x\\ge 2]\\cdot (x-2)^{2}+1[-1\\le x<2]\\cdot 1+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $g(-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 2]=1[-3\\ge 2]=0$ (since $-3\\ge 2$ is false)\n\n- $1[-1\\le x<2]=1[-1\\le -3<2]=0$ (since $-1\\le -3$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =1[-3\\ge 2]\\cdot (-5)^{2}+1[-1\\le -3<2]\\cdot 1+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ only the third term contributes to the result." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-987d0b6b-2b30-5c02-8d2b-78ee1f2cb652"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-987d0b6b-2b30-5c02-8d2b-78ee1f2cb652" :answer-field/id #uuid "987d0b6b-2b30-5c02-8d2b-78ee1f2cb652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-63138a2e-fdf7-5c34-beef-1de60acea7ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c4892a0-601e-5c51-a5e8-9fd4960aaa8a" "answer-416c3487-0d24-54c0-856d-6739a365455d" "answer-e2e3d5db-939c-5344-9198-4a4955c50e69" "answer-63138a2e-fdf7-5c34-beef-1de60acea7ca" "answer-6eade6bb-146e-5007-a93b-9a3431df2347"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c4892a0-601e-5c51-a5e8-9fd4960aaa8a" :answer/id #uuid "0c4892a0-601e-5c51-a5e8-9fd4960aaa8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-416c3487-0d24-54c0-856d-6739a365455d" :answer/id #uuid "416c3487-0d24-54c0-856d-6739a365455d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2e3d5db-939c-5344-9198-4a4955c50e69" :answer/id #uuid "e2e3d5db-939c-5344-9198-4a4955c50e69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63138a2e-fdf7-5c34-beef-1de60acea7ca" :answer/id #uuid "63138a2e-fdf7-5c34-beef-1de60acea7ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6eade6bb-146e-5007-a93b-9a3431df2347" :answer/id #uuid "6eade6bb-146e-5007-a93b-9a3431df2347" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168050 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x \\in A$ and $0$ if $x \\notin A.$ \n![](images/c1/c12d2f59eb7eb9730fc7576bb426cff90a3cff4ac8577c3f212db8101197300b.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we introduce a more general notation that allows us to write indicator functions for any condition, not just set membership.\n\nThe **Iverson bracket notation** $\\mathbf{1}[P]$ takes a predicate $P$ and returns\n![](images/4d/4dbe29e205fecf97bd7d4c0bfbf810c7336a6a50198fa35319e7ae8b0ac505c0.svg)\n\nFor example:\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[3 > 2] = 1$ because $3 > 2$ is true.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[5 \\leq 4] = 0$ because $5 \\leq 4$ is false.\n\n- $\\mathbf{1}[7 \\in \\{2, 5, 7\\}] = 1$ because $7$ is an element of $\\{2, 5, 7\\}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168059 :tutorial/content "The set-based notation from the previous lesson is a special case of Iverson bracket notation. Specifically,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{1}_A(x) = \\mathbf{1}[x \\in A].\n$$\n\nBoth expressions equal $1$ when $x \\in A$ and $0$ when $x \\notin A.$ \n\nThe Iverson bracket is more flexible because it works with any predicate, such as inequalities. For example:\n![](images/cd/cd01819adb510c2b439f44089b1bd3cbd1f49e671779039fdf2f6be62a39810f.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168051 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions are useful for writing expressions that behave differently depending on whether a condition is met.\n\n**Sums with indicator functions.** When we multiply a term by an indicator function inside a sum, the indicator acts as a filter. For example, given values $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ the sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0] \\cdot x_i\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values. If $x_i > 0,$ the indicator equals $1$ and the term contributes $x_i.$ If $x_i \\leq 0,$ the indicator equals $0$ and the term contributes nothing.\n\nConsider the values $x_1 = 5, x_2 = -3, x_3 = 2.$ Using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1[5>0]\\cdot 5+1[-3>0]\\cdot (-3)+1[2>0]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 5+0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+0+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\mathbf{1}[x_i > 0]$ counts the positive values. For this data, the count is $1 + 0 + 1 = 2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168060 :tutorial/content "Indicator functions can also express piecewise functions compactly. Consider the absolute value function:\n![](images/33/336dc8080661db77e7ac701204aa6a32075bc7f4bd06625f4e8467e87a5c075c.svg)\n\nWe can write this as a single expression using indicator functions:\n\n$$\n|x| = \\mathbf{1}[x \\geq 0] \\cdot x + \\mathbf{1}[x < 0] \\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nSince the conditions $x \\geq 0$ and $x < 0$ are mutually exclusive, exactly one indicator equals $1$ and the other equals $0.$ This \"switch\" mechanism selects the appropriate term automatically.\n\nLet's verify this expression for a negative number, say $x = -4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|-4| & =1[-4\\ge 0]\\cdot (-4)+1[-4<0]\\cdot (-(-4)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167001 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100823 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d82dc916-4a3b-544e-8c9e-9820eeb6151d" "question-bacd5fdb-8e88-5e6c-8872-876eba96fadd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d82dc916-4a3b-544e-8c9e-9820eeb6151d" :question/id #uuid "d82dc916-4a3b-544e-8c9e-9820eeb6151d" :question/math-academy-id "q-100057" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dd62794ee072530cd2b6c8028101b58ae617d6757d29f79de90d1a439f6529b.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "We find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ So we calculate\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(6x-1)f(x)}{5} & =\\frac{1}{5}(6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}x-1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(6(1)-1)(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9379bdb2-a24f-536a-8f21-7af1b41bfabf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9379bdb2-a24f-536a-8f21-7af1b41bfabf" :answer-field/id #uuid "9379bdb2-a24f-536a-8f21-7af1b41bfabf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ebf35fc4-0777-595a-b312-adcad1105f1c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39d9a121-2cea-54a4-be8c-879ad88faecb" "answer-ebf35fc4-0777-595a-b312-adcad1105f1c" "answer-19197229-5d48-5afc-abfd-66d923c6020f" "answer-da80b901-76be-5b81-adc9-6cd165130508" "answer-8646dd0f-8b2b-546d-837a-0eb10eef94e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39d9a121-2cea-54a4-be8c-879ad88faecb" :answer/id #uuid "39d9a121-2cea-54a4-be8c-879ad88faecb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebf35fc4-0777-595a-b312-adcad1105f1c" :answer/id #uuid "ebf35fc4-0777-595a-b312-adcad1105f1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19197229-5d48-5afc-abfd-66d923c6020f" :answer/id #uuid "19197229-5d48-5afc-abfd-66d923c6020f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da80b901-76be-5b81-adc9-6cd165130508" :answer/id #uuid "da80b901-76be-5b81-adc9-6cd165130508" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8646dd0f-8b2b-546d-837a-0eb10eef94e9" :answer/id #uuid "8646dd0f-8b2b-546d-837a-0eb10eef94e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100953 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a5e8422b-8be3-5e23-8b9e-af4b866bbaf9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a5e8422b-8be3-5e23-8b9e-af4b866bbaf9" :question/id #uuid "a5e8422b-8be3-5e23-8b9e-af4b866bbaf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100100" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cc4f4447ee6fb212222b28c7e4c31cca435c542e3d2f25ae3f3331a3b98da2b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=6$  and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=2.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))=6+2(2)=10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))=1+2(2)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a6654db-527e-57c6-a150-229af646b191"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a6654db-527e-57c6-a150-229af646b191" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a6654db-527e-57c6-a150-229af646b191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90bb2958-b97e-5235-b2bd-23dc8aa76027" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90bb2958-b97e-5235-b2bd-23dc8aa76027" "answer-71617208-0858-502e-bb7b-40761d03d4d5" "answer-4660fdd8-5261-5c4e-bc67-7363b74699d6" "answer-a2416292-adfa-5246-a3d7-f18168238265" "answer-bcd4397a-616a-58ad-bf05-1dd4260c9cec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90bb2958-b97e-5235-b2bd-23dc8aa76027" :answer/id #uuid "90bb2958-b97e-5235-b2bd-23dc8aa76027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71617208-0858-502e-bb7b-40761d03d4d5" :answer/id #uuid "71617208-0858-502e-bb7b-40761d03d4d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4660fdd8-5261-5c4e-bc67-7363b74699d6" :answer/id #uuid "4660fdd8-5261-5c4e-bc67-7363b74699d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2416292-adfa-5246-a3d7-f18168238265" :answer/id #uuid "a2416292-adfa-5246-a3d7-f18168238265" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcd4397a-616a-58ad-bf05-1dd4260c9cec" :answer/id #uuid "bcd4397a-616a-58ad-bf05-1dd4260c9cec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bacd5fdb-8e88-5e6c-8872-876eba96fadd" :question/id #uuid "bacd5fdb-8e88-5e6c-8872-876eba96fadd" :question/math-academy-id "q-300738" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b563936f37e82c3f6a422f80b448a7902a656a2749a7abced6c7bb9225ecf2bc.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x)=2.$ So, applying the product rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-4)^{2}+1)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =66\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-696cc972-ece8-5b53-8faf-f9002d4c084b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-696cc972-ece8-5b53-8faf-f9002d4c084b" :answer-field/id #uuid "696cc972-ece8-5b53-8faf-f9002d4c084b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ae6f200f-ddfc-5978-9499-832c6010156c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae6f200f-ddfc-5978-9499-832c6010156c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae6f200f-ddfc-5978-9499-832c6010156c" :answer/id #uuid "ae6f200f-ddfc-5978-9499-832c6010156c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "66" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8e49eaa1-6e20-5177-b734-5660533a859b" :activity/id #uuid "8e49eaa1-6e20-5177-b734-5660533a859b" :activity/title "The Product and Quotient Rules for Limits" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049226 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-661325a7-b965-509f-ad3e-d886d2af7b21" "step-e9b4f224-2dd0-5bdc-bfd2-a9d142fc0bad" "step-a83fb1c7-539b-570e-8e59-c06ea48e47e1"] :activity/first-step "step-661325a7-b965-509f-ad3e-d886d2af7b21" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-661325a7-b965-509f-ad3e-d886d2af7b21" :step/id #uuid "661325a7-b965-509f-ad3e-d886d2af7b21" :step/content "question-d82dc916-4a3b-544e-8c9e-9820eeb6151d" :step/next "step-e9b4f224-2dd0-5bdc-bfd2-a9d142fc0bad" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e9b4f224-2dd0-5bdc-bfd2-a9d142fc0bad" :step/id #uuid "e9b4f224-2dd0-5bdc-bfd2-a9d142fc0bad" :step/content "question-a5e8422b-8be3-5e23-8b9e-af4b866bbaf9" :step/next "step-a83fb1c7-539b-570e-8e59-c06ea48e47e1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a83fb1c7-539b-570e-8e59-c06ea48e47e1" :step/id #uuid "a83fb1c7-539b-570e-8e59-c06ea48e47e1" :step/content "question-bacd5fdb-8e88-5e6c-8872-876eba96fadd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186127285 :answer-field/choices 17592186127286] [:db/retract 17592186127285 :answer-field/choices 17592186127287] [:db/retract 17592186127285 :answer-field/choices 17592186127288] [:db/retract 17592186127285 :answer-field/choices 17592186127290] {:db/id 17592186129634 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v) \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2u+v)$ and $y=uv.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2u+v} & \\frac{1}{2u+v} \\\\ \\,\\text{ }v\\, & \\,\\text{ }u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2u+v}\\cdot u-\\frac{1}{2u+v}\\cdot v \\\\  & =\\frac{2u-v}{2u+v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127285 :answer-field/correct "answer-b6ea50ce-74b8-5b02-885a-93867c7a6416" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b6ea50ce-74b8-5b02-885a-93867c7a6416" "answer-15048a11-7e53-5d37-9136-e5f4e214f260" "answer-5f05ebb0-daf9-56de-972b-defa4c733843" "answer-4aface07-79f1-5422-a990-09a984d1e716"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6ea50ce-74b8-5b02-885a-93867c7a6416" :answer/id #uuid "b6ea50ce-74b8-5b02-885a-93867c7a6416" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2u-v}{2u+v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15048a11-7e53-5d37-9136-e5f4e214f260" :answer/id #uuid "15048a11-7e53-5d37-9136-e5f4e214f260" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2v-u}{2u+v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f05ebb0-daf9-56de-972b-defa4c733843" :answer/id #uuid "5f05ebb0-daf9-56de-972b-defa4c733843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{u+v}{2u+v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4aface07-79f1-5422-a990-09a984d1e716" :answer/id #uuid "4aface07-79f1-5422-a990-09a984d1e716" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{u-v}{2u+v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8c92f7d0-e8c8-58c1-802d-bfc2ba502311" :activity/id #uuid "8c92f7d0-e8c8-58c1-802d-bfc2ba502311" :activity/title "The Jacobian" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048219 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-be00d625-1070-56aa-80f7-076e80fdefca" "step-bdbbcc20-9236-5241-8d61-c01a918e3318" "step-c7b74295-d1b7-5c30-bae5-69ba002b04d6" "step-6581def3-549b-570e-8207-6ebb39c64d70" "step-8077f706-e34e-57b9-bdbc-aa14a025b061"] :activity/first-step "step-be00d625-1070-56aa-80f7-076e80fdefca" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-be00d625-1070-56aa-80f7-076e80fdefca" :step/id #uuid "be00d625-1070-56aa-80f7-076e80fdefca" :step/content 17592186129634 :step/next "step-bdbbcc20-9236-5241-8d61-c01a918e3318" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bdbbcc20-9236-5241-8d61-c01a918e3318" :step/id #uuid "bdbbcc20-9236-5241-8d61-c01a918e3318" :step/content 17592186129503 :step/next "step-c7b74295-d1b7-5c30-bae5-69ba002b04d6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c7b74295-d1b7-5c30-bae5-69ba002b04d6" :step/id #uuid "c7b74295-d1b7-5c30-bae5-69ba002b04d6" :step/content 17592186278288 :step/next "step-6581def3-549b-570e-8207-6ebb39c64d70" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6581def3-549b-570e-8207-6ebb39c64d70" :step/id #uuid "6581def3-549b-570e-8207-6ebb39c64d70" :step/content 17592186278300 :step/next "step-8077f706-e34e-57b9-bdbc-aa14a025b061" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8077f706-e34e-57b9-bdbc-aa14a025b061" :step/id #uuid "8077f706-e34e-57b9-bdbc-aa14a025b061" :step/content 17592186383568 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186061897 :answer-field/choices 17592186061898] [:db/retract 17592186061897 :answer-field/choices 17592186061899] [:db/retract 17592186061897 :answer-field/choices 17592186061900] [:db/retract 17592186061897 :answer-field/choices 17592186061901] [:db/retract 17592186061897 :answer-field/choices 17592186061902] {:db/id 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4eddc77-b22f-562d-ab08-be01c533b457" "question-8cec0878-9352-5be3-a087-f066a0b92d19" "question-b66e27f7-fcba-5767-8cef-431ef74750b6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9a2565d5-0f88-53cc-81b1-7e0ad2dcfafe" "question-5e8039e3-82ac-52bc-a02f-bab2dbe2062b" "question-6378a8a6-ced0-5ab5-b937-c9000ce1db16"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5082ae1f-b15c-594a-83e6-841946d45040" "question-08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" "question-0f159c95-e1be-591f-b6cb-f9ebf9404573"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" "question-c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" "question-dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" "question-9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064640 :question/problem "![](images/74/740d181b8e8eff44f0eb258c8b68f30d05f70445aefabd5c00fd68e570e825dd.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $3\\text{ mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the sides have a length of $12\\text{ mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2(12)=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $72\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061897 :answer-field/correct "answer-8773b3bc-ffa1-58c8-9d06-4516207829f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6240a033-4475-5d68-a24e-51e9eb3191cf" "answer-3f08dcdb-af34-5a27-8c71-56b58794b8e3" "answer-635c658c-f5ae-5a89-ba69-4a562ce5f220" "answer-8773b3bc-ffa1-58c8-9d06-4516207829f7" "answer-954a78d3-b868-53eb-ad2d-108c5bf1d930"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6240a033-4475-5d68-a24e-51e9eb3191cf" :answer/id #uuid "6240a033-4475-5d68-a24e-51e9eb3191cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "144{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f08dcdb-af34-5a27-8c71-56b58794b8e3" :answer/id #uuid "3f08dcdb-af34-5a27-8c71-56b58794b8e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-635c658c-f5ae-5a89-ba69-4a562ce5f220" :answer/id #uuid "635c658c-f5ae-5a89-ba69-4a562ce5f220" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8773b3bc-ffa1-58c8-9d06-4516207829f7" :answer/id #uuid "8773b3bc-ffa1-58c8-9d06-4516207829f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-954a78d3-b868-53eb-ad2d-108c5bf1d930" :answer/id #uuid "954a78d3-b868-53eb-ad2d-108c5bf1d930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4eddc77-b22f-562d-ab08-be01c533b457" :question/id #uuid "e4eddc77-b22f-562d-ab08-be01c533b457" :question/math-academy-id "q-2773" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd63afc83917b365d1b91bf4b033be10b64c76a18e7aa16cb8019dc8ef197eb1.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $6\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $4\\text{ cm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(6)\\cdot 2(4)=48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-205c8531-1b66-5ba4-8976-46d4ed255c28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-205c8531-1b66-5ba4-8976-46d4ed255c28" :answer-field/id #uuid "205c8531-1b66-5ba4-8976-46d4ed255c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54f0f721-c507-5e47-bac7-834e87887094" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a3fe0de0-f7c7-5ab2-ac5b-527ba64fd164" "answer-23286283-12a6-5c29-ac96-a63e013fea9d" "answer-84d32b58-5c12-5e75-9b6a-b4f0565bb964" "answer-52280bf1-eb40-5698-9eab-29a3665e6262" "answer-54f0f721-c507-5e47-bac7-834e87887094"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3fe0de0-f7c7-5ab2-ac5b-527ba64fd164" :answer/id #uuid "a3fe0de0-f7c7-5ab2-ac5b-527ba64fd164" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23286283-12a6-5c29-ac96-a63e013fea9d" :answer/id #uuid "23286283-12a6-5c29-ac96-a63e013fea9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84d32b58-5c12-5e75-9b6a-b4f0565bb964" :answer/id #uuid "84d32b58-5c12-5e75-9b6a-b4f0565bb964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52280bf1-eb40-5698-9eab-29a3665e6262" :answer/id #uuid "52280bf1-eb40-5698-9eab-29a3665e6262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54f0f721-c507-5e47-bac7-834e87887094" :answer/id #uuid "54f0f721-c507-5e47-bac7-834e87887094" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8cec0878-9352-5be3-a087-f066a0b92d19" :question/id #uuid "8cec0878-9352-5be3-a087-f066a0b92d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-115924" :question/problem "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to ten times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the length of the sides of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, at that particular moment we have $x=5\\text{ m}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b962a43a-d4a9-571b-a6b2-7e3f1cbc1ef3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b962a43a-d4a9-571b-a6b2-7e3f1cbc1ef3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b962a43a-d4a9-571b-a6b2-7e3f1cbc1ef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ddd82a8-41d8-5633-b335-922238ee66a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa4a3b6b-fdc1-5d0c-89ef-a7234b4f419c" "answer-251d3cf9-4f92-5be8-aa35-c4057d43ad83" "answer-3be5b30f-4289-592e-8f61-90413aa1af64" "answer-1c35528d-6a69-5348-80f8-4fb876374bd0" "answer-3ddd82a8-41d8-5633-b335-922238ee66a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa4a3b6b-fdc1-5d0c-89ef-a7234b4f419c" :answer/id #uuid "fa4a3b6b-fdc1-5d0c-89ef-a7234b4f419c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-251d3cf9-4f92-5be8-aa35-c4057d43ad83" :answer/id #uuid "251d3cf9-4f92-5be8-aa35-c4057d43ad83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3be5b30f-4289-592e-8f61-90413aa1af64" :answer/id #uuid "3be5b30f-4289-592e-8f61-90413aa1af64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c35528d-6a69-5348-80f8-4fb876374bd0" :answer/id #uuid "1c35528d-6a69-5348-80f8-4fb876374bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ddd82a8-41d8-5633-b335-922238ee66a5" :answer/id #uuid "3ddd82a8-41d8-5633-b335-922238ee66a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\text{ m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b66e27f7-fcba-5767-8cef-431ef74750b6" :question/id #uuid "b66e27f7-fcba-5767-8cef-431ef74750b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-314491" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8944ed32d3019a0d0d1ccebbc2f40c5248c221f3cddba237906db509fac0737.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $8\\text{ ft/s}.$ At what rate is the area of ​​the square increasing when the side is $4\\,\\text{ft}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{ft/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{ft},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\cdot 2(4) \\\\  & =64{\\text{ ft}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0cbefda-a203-5ea4-914e-7a55a9ace9c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0cbefda-a203-5ea4-914e-7a55a9ace9c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0cbefda-a203-5ea4-914e-7a55a9ace9c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2b7a1700-34a9-5048-a8fc-580d0f5f6abb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b7a1700-34a9-5048-a8fc-580d0f5f6abb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b7a1700-34a9-5048-a8fc-580d0f5f6abb" :answer/id #uuid "2b7a1700-34a9-5048-a8fc-580d0f5f6abb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a2565d5-0f88-53cc-81b1-7e0ad2dcfafe" :question/id #uuid "9a2565d5-0f88-53cc-81b1-7e0ad2dcfafe" :question/math-academy-id "q-73320" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f4af299-5b93-51eb-b385-3f0f4ad159a9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f4af299-5b93-51eb-b385-3f0f4ad159a9" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f4af299-5b93-51eb-b385-3f0f4ad159a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a639175-1465-5a64-b32f-cf91e2f5edec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a656bd00-67bb-5523-830c-b4b23913621b" "answer-4a639175-1465-5a64-b32f-cf91e2f5edec" "answer-ea6596e5-4bc0-5c9f-8401-1fc0a34be568" "answer-d75272db-a98d-5628-8afb-0b5d34f9ad85" "answer-0ae14c7f-afe1-5af8-a226-578eb25a1d6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a656bd00-67bb-5523-830c-b4b23913621b" :answer/id #uuid "a656bd00-67bb-5523-830c-b4b23913621b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a639175-1465-5a64-b32f-cf91e2f5edec" :answer/id #uuid "4a639175-1465-5a64-b32f-cf91e2f5edec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea6596e5-4bc0-5c9f-8401-1fc0a34be568" :answer/id #uuid "ea6596e5-4bc0-5c9f-8401-1fc0a34be568" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d75272db-a98d-5628-8afb-0b5d34f9ad85" :answer/id #uuid "d75272db-a98d-5628-8afb-0b5d34f9ad85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ae14c7f-afe1-5af8-a226-578eb25a1d6d" :answer/id #uuid "0ae14c7f-afe1-5af8-a226-578eb25a1d6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5e8039e3-82ac-52bc-a02f-bab2dbe2062b" :question/id #uuid "5e8039e3-82ac-52bc-a02f-bab2dbe2062b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344456" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=24\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6517593-c488-552c-8243-888093248acc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6517593-c488-552c-8243-888093248acc" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6517593-c488-552c-8243-888093248acc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-17145308-c3c7-5435-ad19-e0ed2a06df6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17145308-c3c7-5435-ad19-e0ed2a06df6c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17145308-c3c7-5435-ad19-e0ed2a06df6c" :answer/id #uuid "17145308-c3c7-5435-ad19-e0ed2a06df6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6378a8a6-ced0-5ab5-b937-c9000ce1db16" :question/id #uuid "6378a8a6-ced0-5ab5-b937-c9000ce1db16" :question/math-academy-id "q-344457" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $20{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=20\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-12122b0d-d6c1-5e9e-83ff-746168fe72f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-12122b0d-d6c1-5e9e-83ff-746168fe72f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "12122b0d-d6c1-5e9e-83ff-746168fe72f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-960848f1-cdc7-5fcf-a7f2-24297ee9fc40" :answer-field/choices ["answer-960848f1-cdc7-5fcf-a7f2-24297ee9fc40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-960848f1-cdc7-5fcf-a7f2-24297ee9fc40" :answer/id #uuid "960848f1-cdc7-5fcf-a7f2-24297ee9fc40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5082ae1f-b15c-594a-83e6-841946d45040" :question/id #uuid "5082ae1f-b15c-594a-83e6-841946d45040" :question/math-academy-id "q-116049" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbd79b56420475595834e8cd67d4b9d3d5ca8a2e0fda399f49de1f4e9bb26c95.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square increase at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour}$, at what rate is the perimeter changing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(4\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=16\\text{ feet/hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square increases at $16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dad03ac8-e6b0-55d6-a367-f056cb5c48e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dad03ac8-e6b0-55d6-a367-f056cb5c48e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "dad03ac8-e6b0-55d6-a367-f056cb5c48e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5800a282-089f-5bee-b27e-6bfc03da7f2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5800a282-089f-5bee-b27e-6bfc03da7f2c" "answer-131bb04e-bc65-52c2-a042-ead28c18bb28" "answer-357f7502-20f6-552a-bf7e-48abdb63a59e" "answer-58527ab4-efe9-53d9-9523-50427c75098c" "answer-8ba663cb-d89a-5b2b-94de-62b822f02bab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5800a282-089f-5bee-b27e-6bfc03da7f2c" :answer/id #uuid "5800a282-089f-5bee-b27e-6bfc03da7f2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-131bb04e-bc65-52c2-a042-ead28c18bb28" :answer/id #uuid "131bb04e-bc65-52c2-a042-ead28c18bb28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-357f7502-20f6-552a-bf7e-48abdb63a59e" :answer/id #uuid "357f7502-20f6-552a-bf7e-48abdb63a59e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58527ab4-efe9-53d9-9523-50427c75098c" :answer/id #uuid "58527ab4-efe9-53d9-9523-50427c75098c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ba663cb-d89a-5b2b-94de-62b822f02bab" :answer/id #uuid "8ba663cb-d89a-5b2b-94de-62b822f02bab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" :question/id #uuid "08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" :question/math-academy-id "q-24805" :question/problem "![](images/19/197557b866b4a4a1c2c151c29cc529e12a5048b941223fe8e2eb4a7c4959223e.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square increase in length at a rate of $1.5\\text{ mm/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48f0d76a-3c1e-522a-8915-c0902dc2770d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48f0d76a-3c1e-522a-8915-c0902dc2770d" :answer-field/id #uuid "48f0d76a-3c1e-522a-8915-c0902dc2770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba4f4407-5a99-5b48-917e-de381c7c9cde" "answer-2186a87e-24dc-58c2-a7ab-6583096c289d" "answer-0c44563a-1326-5d99-9881-696a2bb3906d" "answer-5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" "answer-ec960d32-a56e-5be1-a636-fdd4c8d72cf3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba4f4407-5a99-5b48-917e-de381c7c9cde" :answer/id #uuid "ba4f4407-5a99-5b48-917e-de381c7c9cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2186a87e-24dc-58c2-a7ab-6583096c289d" :answer/id #uuid "2186a87e-24dc-58c2-a7ab-6583096c289d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c44563a-1326-5d99-9881-696a2bb3906d" :answer/id #uuid "0c44563a-1326-5d99-9881-696a2bb3906d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer/id #uuid "5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec960d32-a56e-5be1-a636-fdd4c8d72cf3" :answer/id #uuid "ec960d32-a56e-5be1-a636-fdd4c8d72cf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f159c95-e1be-591f-b6cb-f9ebf9404573" :question/id #uuid "0f159c95-e1be-591f-b6cb-f9ebf9404573" :question/math-academy-id "q-116055" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb40860015e5404f3680b22835149ddc63baf80cd8e1f34e30f936308c3221e0.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-40\\text{ mm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-84dd87d0-922b-58de-8a95-b91eeb107edc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-84dd87d0-922b-58de-8a95-b91eeb107edc" :answer-field/id #uuid "84dd87d0-922b-58de-8a95-b91eeb107edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a1efe3d-09db-5caa-8705-9b5fa2d74f19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb0ed7e5-09ed-5b08-ab64-ff34690c3ecc" "answer-2f5f17d2-7461-58c3-870a-3459da5761ed" "answer-b2ae7e2c-192a-5e5d-af15-24061dc8c47e" "answer-7a1efe3d-09db-5caa-8705-9b5fa2d74f19" "answer-24c81138-05fa-586e-96b9-07589b7d7c89"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb0ed7e5-09ed-5b08-ab64-ff34690c3ecc" :answer/id #uuid "eb0ed7e5-09ed-5b08-ab64-ff34690c3ecc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5f17d2-7461-58c3-870a-3459da5761ed" :answer/id #uuid "2f5f17d2-7461-58c3-870a-3459da5761ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2ae7e2c-192a-5e5d-af15-24061dc8c47e" :answer/id #uuid "b2ae7e2c-192a-5e5d-af15-24061dc8c47e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a1efe3d-09db-5caa-8705-9b5fa2d74f19" :answer/id #uuid "7a1efe3d-09db-5caa-8705-9b5fa2d74f19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24c81138-05fa-586e-96b9-07589b7d7c89" :answer/id #uuid "24c81138-05fa-586e-96b9-07589b7d7c89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" :question/id #uuid "37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" :question/math-academy-id "q-314510" :question/problem "![](images/00/007ac521cd9bd97773aa18e243e788ce9eb473a65e3242022539266bc8f23ef4.png)\n\nA laser technician is reducing the diagonals of a square aperture at a rate of $2.4\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the aperture decreasing when the diagonals are $12.5\\,\\text{mm}$ long each?\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=12.5.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4\\cdot 12.5=-30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square aperture is decreasing at a rate of $30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3836a26d-16ce-5b20-91e1-5b41083d18ec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3836a26d-16ce-5b20-91e1-5b41083d18ec" :answer-field/id #uuid "3836a26d-16ce-5b20-91e1-5b41083d18ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer/id #uuid "8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186500218 :answer-field/correct "answer-11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/id #uuid "11f56a07-742f-503a-bfa2-c32cba6871e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" :question/id #uuid "c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73369" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b4196d82bf27236e6c28651a01678c60c41bc851afbb7aad2e754578ba330e9.png)\n\nJacob is a carpenter. He wishes to reduce the size of a wooden square while maintaining its shape. The lengths of the diagonals of the square decrease at a rate of $2\\text{ cm}\\text{/h}.$ At what rate, in squared centimeters per hour, is the area of ​​the square decreasing when the length of the diagonal is $45\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/h},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=45.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 45=-90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0a87e9bd-e6be-51a6-8fce-13d2940093e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0a87e9bd-e6be-51a6-8fce-13d2940093e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0a87e9bd-e6be-51a6-8fce-13d2940093e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-93b930d8-b345-570b-a7c7-dc0aff86eabe" "answer-2f35a4a7-d9a4-52b8-80c9-e437acc5e057" "answer-6b377338-8877-5e4e-8347-0093dfa4516c" "answer-ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" "answer-059a746a-7219-54b6-8c45-7f39d3a7b05a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93b930d8-b345-570b-a7c7-dc0aff86eabe" :answer/id #uuid "93b930d8-b345-570b-a7c7-dc0aff86eabe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f35a4a7-d9a4-52b8-80c9-e437acc5e057" :answer/id #uuid "2f35a4a7-d9a4-52b8-80c9-e437acc5e057" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22.5\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b377338-8877-5e4e-8347-0093dfa4516c" :answer/id #uuid "6b377338-8877-5e4e-8347-0093dfa4516c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "47\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer/id #uuid "ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-059a746a-7219-54b6-8c45-7f39d3a7b05a" :answer/id #uuid "059a746a-7219-54b6-8c45-7f39d3a7b05a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "93\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" :question/id #uuid "dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" :question/math-academy-id "q-344649" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb048e7ac152ffbc740d611726871f395b4c6c4abcc324a4eed890e6116ec814.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually increases the size of a square opening by lengthening its diagonals at a rate of $1.6\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening increasing when each diagonal measures $25\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $1.6\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=25.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =1.6\\cdot 25=40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square opening is increasing at a rate of \n$40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bc6154d-11f3-5894-9fac-15ff31065158"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bc6154d-11f3-5894-9fac-15ff31065158" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc6154d-11f3-5894-9fac-15ff31065158" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer/id #uuid "5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8" :question/id #uuid "9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344674" :question/problem "![](images/34/34a41e39a36d3e073ef2ca4144bd346b340efa22a9da7d3e8a9df48d32d900b3.png)\n\nA square opening in a testing chamber is being reduced, causing the area of the opening to decrease at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals decreasing when each diagonal measures $35\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square opening, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-49.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=35.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{35}\\cdot (-49) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{49}{35} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.4\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the opening is decreasing at a rate of \n$1.4\\text{ mm/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-046001e6-52ad-564f-b292-d2455a9b9819"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-046001e6-52ad-564f-b292-d2455a9b9819" :answer-field/id #uuid "046001e6-52ad-564f-b292-d2455a9b9819" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer/id #uuid "61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083217 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5bf1583d-22a0-55d5-823f-9b4dd8cd99b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083017 :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120.\n$$\n\n- Computing $2!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n2! = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5!\\,2! & =5!\\cdot 2! \\\\  & =120\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =240\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083335 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9ddd958f-3ed8-5647-8898-4f321748c5a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083137 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{5!}{0!}.$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a quotient of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5!}{0!} & =\\frac{120}{1}=120\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083218 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4e0bd81c-fffc-5b90-b2d5-10d251044393" "question-3e9e4e75-7918-575f-a18d-8c831116251f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083018 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {5!} {2!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $5! = 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2! \\, .$ Then, we can cancel the $2!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {5!} {2!} \n&= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2!} {2!} \\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot  \\cancel{2!}} {\\cancel{2!}} \\[5pt]\n&= \t\t\\dfrac {5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3} {1} \\[5pt]\n&= \n\t\t60\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bf1583d-22a0-55d5-823f-9b4dd8cd99b3" :question/id #uuid "5bf1583d-22a0-55d5-823f-9b4dd8cd99b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122025" :question/problem "$9!=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing $9!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n9!=9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=362\\,880.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7742e3f-6c18-573d-b40a-6b9885cf2856"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7742e3f-6c18-573d-b40a-6b9885cf2856" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7742e3f-6c18-573d-b40a-6b9885cf2856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b9d3a921-84b6-5973-90d4-323de837035b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e93959a6-6322-56d2-a4a1-532787d89221" "answer-b9d3a921-84b6-5973-90d4-323de837035b" "answer-f54f2cd6-56b9-59bd-98f1-25f8225ccde2" "answer-c40a534d-df67-5fc4-b3d3-ddc328582726" "answer-ca73191b-b2e3-5dbb-8810-8b286230a0c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e93959a6-6322-56d2-a4a1-532787d89221" :answer/id #uuid "e93959a6-6322-56d2-a4a1-532787d89221" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "181\\,440" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9d3a921-84b6-5973-90d4-323de837035b" :answer/id #uuid "b9d3a921-84b6-5973-90d4-323de837035b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "362\\,880" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f54f2cd6-56b9-59bd-98f1-25f8225ccde2" :answer/id #uuid "f54f2cd6-56b9-59bd-98f1-25f8225ccde2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,024" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c40a534d-df67-5fc4-b3d3-ddc328582726" :answer/id #uuid "c40a534d-df67-5fc4-b3d3-ddc328582726" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\,320" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca73191b-b2e3-5dbb-8810-8b286230a0c5" :answer/id #uuid "ca73191b-b2e3-5dbb-8810-8b286230a0c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\,480" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083926 :question/problem "$2!\\,3!=$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $2!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n2!=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Computing $3!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n3!=3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2!\\,3! & =2!\\cdot 3! \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082487 :answer-field/correct 17592186082489 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ddd958f-3ed8-5647-8898-4f321748c5a0" :question/id #uuid "9ddd958f-3ed8-5647-8898-4f321748c5a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124397" :question/problem "$\\frac{4!}{0!}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is a quotient of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $4!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n4!=4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=24.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4!}{0!} & =\\frac{24}{1}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-62aef874-cc70-57c0-a86f-213a5e547b1e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-62aef874-cc70-57c0-a86f-213a5e547b1e" :answer-field/id #uuid "62aef874-cc70-57c0-a86f-213a5e547b1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55e685f8-bad2-5f95-839b-b0d5a59b119b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba43c4a7-afeb-55b3-8b6e-d5befe33c1d5" "answer-b256b170-fab8-5064-a927-c0efbcd4ac49" "answer-55e685f8-bad2-5f95-839b-b0d5a59b119b" "answer-4574eac6-83c8-5a3b-8f18-e62b91c0b334" "answer-d20163ce-a206-50ee-a8ef-34d27bb08bae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba43c4a7-afeb-55b3-8b6e-d5befe33c1d5" :answer/id #uuid "ba43c4a7-afeb-55b3-8b6e-d5befe33c1d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b256b170-fab8-5064-a927-c0efbcd4ac49" :answer/id #uuid "b256b170-fab8-5064-a927-c0efbcd4ac49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55e685f8-bad2-5f95-839b-b0d5a59b119b" :answer/id #uuid "55e685f8-bad2-5f95-839b-b0d5a59b119b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4574eac6-83c8-5a3b-8f18-e62b91c0b334" :answer/id #uuid "4574eac6-83c8-5a3b-8f18-e62b91c0b334" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d20163ce-a206-50ee-a8ef-34d27bb08bae" :answer/id #uuid "d20163ce-a206-50ee-a8ef-34d27bb08bae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4e0bd81c-fffc-5b90-b2d5-10d251044393" :question/id #uuid "4e0bd81c-fffc-5b90-b2d5-10d251044393" :question/math-academy-id "q-73019" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{8!\\,5!}{3!\\,4!\\,6!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $8!=8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6!$ and $5!=5\\cdot 4!$ . Then, we can cancel the $6!$ and $4!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8!\\,5!}{3!\\,4!\\,6!} & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 6!\\cdot 5\\cdot 4!}{3!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{3!\\cdot \\cancel{4!}\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 7\\cdot 5}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{140}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-38b7fe1e-34c3-508a-8ffa-6a2b08675b26"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-38b7fe1e-34c3-508a-8ffa-6a2b08675b26" :answer-field/id #uuid "38b7fe1e-34c3-508a-8ffa-6a2b08675b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e1fcab41-7953-57bc-bd9e-0e87d2ab73d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e1fcab41-7953-57bc-bd9e-0e87d2ab73d2" "answer-6a9bb3aa-6b51-58d8-b1af-91bd45411a7b" "answer-931dbc23-be9e-59e3-b1e8-6992aca17d8a" "answer-fc98e19a-9d26-58be-a66b-509f498db309" "answer-4460ffde-f366-5f49-aab2-737efad5edb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1fcab41-7953-57bc-bd9e-0e87d2ab73d2" :answer/id #uuid "e1fcab41-7953-57bc-bd9e-0e87d2ab73d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{140}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9bb3aa-6b51-58d8-b1af-91bd45411a7b" :answer/id #uuid "6a9bb3aa-6b51-58d8-b1af-91bd45411a7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{35}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-931dbc23-be9e-59e3-b1e8-6992aca17d8a" :answer/id #uuid "931dbc23-be9e-59e3-b1e8-6992aca17d8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc98e19a-9d26-58be-a66b-509f498db309" :answer/id #uuid "fc98e19a-9d26-58be-a66b-509f498db309" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4460ffde-f366-5f49-aab2-737efad5edb6" :answer/id #uuid "4460ffde-f366-5f49-aab2-737efad5edb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{70}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e9e4e75-7918-575f-a18d-8c831116251f" :question/id #uuid "3e9e4e75-7918-575f-a18d-8c831116251f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19873" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{89!\\,82!}{90!\\,81!}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $90!=90\\cdot 89!$ and $82!=82\\cdot 81!\\,.$ Then, we can cancel the $89!$ and $81!$ that occur in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{89!\\,82!}{90!\\,81!} & =\\frac{89!\\,\\cdot 82\\cdot 81!}{90\\cdot 89!\\,\\cdot 81!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{89!\\,}\\cdot 82\\cdot \\cancel{81!}}{90\\cdot \\cancel{89!\\,}\\cdot \\cancel{81!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{90} \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d1abfb3-22d4-511b-944b-e2271fb0f05d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d1abfb3-22d4-511b-944b-e2271fb0f05d" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1abfb3-22d4-511b-944b-e2271fb0f05d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4bae973c-05f8-5a5a-8c78-b86861e2dead" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9ebab680-be6e-5ffe-9c35-9bf5106eba27" "answer-410a4aff-aef1-5924-8451-5151ee0e274c" "answer-4bae973c-05f8-5a5a-8c78-b86861e2dead" "answer-0003b6a0-5b7b-5381-804b-be2455c24eb8" "answer-70c9be2b-110b-59cb-a769-21670ee903ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ebab680-be6e-5ffe-9c35-9bf5106eba27" :answer/id #uuid "9ebab680-be6e-5ffe-9c35-9bf5106eba27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{31}{35}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-410a4aff-aef1-5924-8451-5151ee0e274c" :answer/id #uuid "410a4aff-aef1-5924-8451-5151ee0e274c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{37}{39}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bae973c-05f8-5a5a-8c78-b86861e2dead" :answer/id #uuid "4bae973c-05f8-5a5a-8c78-b86861e2dead" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{41}{45}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0003b6a0-5b7b-5381-804b-be2455c24eb8" :answer/id #uuid "0003b6a0-5b7b-5381-804b-be2455c24eb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{41}{55}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70c9be2b-110b-59cb-a769-21670ee903ec" :answer/id #uuid "70c9be2b-110b-59cb-a769-21670ee903ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{42}{47}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084444 :tutorial/content "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4!$ (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24.\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t1! &= 1 \\[5pt]\n\t\t2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\[5pt]\t\n\t\t5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084445 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results. \n\nFor example, computing $\\dfrac{10!}{8!}$ using this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90.\n\t}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way! \n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot  \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1} } { \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1} } = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90\n\t}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that $10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8!$ and then cancel the $8!$ that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083457 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
